- [x] ALL LANES / INTEGRATOR | **THE HOOK PATH RESOLVES FROM A SYMBOL TABLE EMBEDDED INSIDE dusklight.exe — which is why "any user with any dusklight version" WORKS, and it also names the one build flag that would kill it dead (Housing, §953 first-use ask).** §948 verified my three symbols against `build/windows-msvc-relwithdebinfo/dusklight_exports.def`. **That was the wrong artifact for this question and I am correcting it before anyone designs on it.** `.def`/`dusklight_imports.lib` is the LINK surface (what `FEATURES game` links a mod against). By-name hook resolution does not read it at all: `HookService::resolve` -> `manifest::resolve` (src/dusk/mods/manifest.cpp), and `manifest::initialize` (:242) reads a **symgen blob at `imageBase + s_symdbDescriptor.rva`** — a section INSIDE the running image, embedded by a POST_BUILD step (`symgen manifest <input> --embed $<TARGET_FILE:dusklight>`, cmake/SymbolManifest.cmake:128), validated against that image's own PDB GUID+age. The code says so itself: *"The manifest travels inside the image it describes."* (Note for whoever reads the header: `manifest.hpp`'s comment "Maps the symbol manifest next to the game binary" is STALE — there is no such file; I looked for it and there is none in the build dir because there is nothing to leave behind.) **WHY THIS IS THE ANSWER TO THE USER'S PRODUCT DEFINITION, stated plainly:** a plugin using `DEFINE_HOOK_SYMBOL` bakes **no addresses and ships no symbol file**. It names symbols as strings; the addresses come from whatever dusklight the user is running, keyed to that exe's own build id. **So the plugin is version-independent BY CONSTRUCTION, not by anyone maintaining a compatibility table** — "download the plugin, point it at your ISO, it connects to your dusklight" is mechanically what this design does. That is a stronger delivery story than §934 was able to claim, and it is structural rather than incidental. **THE ONE GATE, and it belongs to whoever cuts releases:** `manifest.cpp:252` — if `s_symdbDescriptor.rva == 0` the host logs *"no symbol manifest embedded; by-name resolution unavailable"* and **EVERY by-name hook in EVERY plugin fails at once, silently from the user's side.** `DUSK_ENABLE_CODE_MODS` defaults **ON** (CMakeLists.txt:208) and SymbolManifest.cmake:60 makes symgen FATAL when required, so our builds are fine. **But SymbolManifest.cmake:64 has a QUIET path: when no symgen prebuilt exists for the host it emits a STATUS line and `return()`s** — configure succeeds, the exe builds, and by-name resolution is dead with no error anywhere near the failure. **RECOMMENDATION (Integrator's call, not mine to land): any dusklight build intended for users must be verified to CARRY the embedded manifest, and that check belongs in the release step, not in a plugin.** A plugin cannot detect this until first use, and by then the user sees "the mod does nothing". **THE PROBE THAT CONVERTS THE REST — LANDED DARK, mod-side, per §939:** `mods-src/ww_hook_smoke/` (main.cpp + CMakeLists.txt + mod.json) + a root guard at CMakeLists.txt behind **`DUSK_WW_HOOK_SMOKE`, default OFF**, so a normal build never configures it. It is an INSTRUMENT, not product: one pre-hook returning `HOOK_CONTINUE` and one post-hook that only reads — zero behaviour change, delete the directory when banked. It tests TEN discriminators in one run rather than "did it fire": mangled-name resolve status, display-name resolve status, symbol flags, **do the two name forms agree on one address**, install/add_pre/add_post results, does the detour actually FIRE, is the `s16 profname` argument marshalled correctly, **did the original still run under HOOK_CONTINUE and is its return value intact**, a fired/never-fired summary at frame 600 so "installed but silent" is visible rather than absent, and H10 on the artifact. **Crucially it distinguishes `MOD_UNSUPPORTED` (no manifest embedded — the gate above) from `MOD_UNAVAILABLE` (symbol genuinely missing): different bugs, different owners, and they must never be reported as one line.** **GATE STATUS: gate-0-B CLEAN** — no `files.cmake` entry, no new seam in any native TP file; the only in-tree edit is a default-OFF `add_subdirectory` guard mirroring the existing `ww_donor_disc` one. **NOT compile_gated, and I am saying why rather than skipping quietly: `tools/foundry/compile_gate.py` is a RECEIVER-TU instrument** — tier 1 indexes identifiers against the receiver tree (a mod legitimately references SDK identifiers that are not there) and tier 2 borrows its flag template from `d_a_bg.cpp.obj`, which carries the exe's includes and defines, not the SDK's. Both would produce confident nonsense on a mod TU. **I ran the correctly-parameterized equivalent instead: a real `cl /Zs` syntax+semantic pass with the mod's own flags** (`/std:c++20 /EHsc /utf-8 /bigobj /DDUSK_MOD_FEATURE_GAME=1 /I sdk\include`, taken from cmake/GameABIConfig.cmake:41/58 and ModSDK.cmake:164) — **EXITCODE=0, clean.** `/Zs` writes no object, so §839 is untouched. **A GAP compile_gate does not cover and I would rather flag than paper over: `DEFINE_HOOK_SYMBOL` signatures are explicitly NOT compiler-checked** (hook.hpp:117 says so). I wrote `void*(short)` for `fpcPf_Get(s16) -> const process_profile_definition*`; that is right for every target we build, but it is a hand-checked claim, not a verified one, and the same is true of every future by-name hook. **ONE COST MEASURED, since it contradicts a design choice already on record:** `hook.hpp:3` hard-errors without `DUSK_MOD_FEATURE_GAME`, so **any hooking plugin MUST take `FEATURES game`** — which on Windows links it against `dusklight_imports.lib` (ModSDK.cmake:246-257). That is precisely the coupling `ww_donor_disc`'s banner was written to avoid (*"Services only — no FEATURES game ... works regardless of build order"*). **I believe the coupling is BUILD-ORDER ONLY** — `DEFINE_HOOK_SYMBOL` emits no link-time reference to any game symbol, so the DLL should carry no import thunk into the exe — **but that is hypothesis H10 and I have NOT verified it; it is checkable in one command on the built artifact (`dumpbin /imports ww_hook_smoke.dll`) and I am asking for it rather than assuming it.** If H10 fails, hooking plugins are exe-version-coupled at LINK time and the version-independence story above is narrower than it looks — which is exactly why it is stated as a hypothesis and not folded into the good news. **QUEUED to BUILD-QUEUE; not built — §839, the Integrator is the only builder.** | tale §953 WHOSE-TURN (Foundry), Housing | 2026-08-14 -> **CONFIRMED AT RUNTIME, ON THE VANILLA EXE — your structural claim now has a receipt, from a run that already happened.** The clean-room run (row 114, log 145418, `Build: rev 95608438c1`, stock upstream Jul 19) logged: `symbol manifest loaded: 288472 symbols, build id 7a48b338b30c4dde8684273d530f660202000000`. Our own run (012809) logged `294750 symbols, build id fb887b64605e4d89b63356d2513450e64c030000`. **Zero `no symbol manifest embedded` / `by-name resolution unavailable` warnings in EITHER.** So: the stock exe carries its own embedded manifest, keyed to its own build id, and our plugin ran against it successfully — **version-independence is not a prediction, it is a thing that already happened on a month-old binary nobody built for us.** Two extra things the pair of logs gives you that neither row had: **(1) THE BUILD ID IS THE PDB IDENTITY.** Our manifest id decodes as GUID `fb887b64605e4d89b63356d2513450e6` + `4c030000` little-endian = 0x34C = **844** — exactly what Foundry's `build_identity.py current` reports (`age 844`). **§953's manifest identity and §945's PDB identity are THE SAME IDENTITY**, so the archive-at-build-time fix and the by-name hook path are guarding one artifact, not two. Worth saying to Foundry before they design the archive around a separate key. **(2) SYMBOL-COUNT DELTA: 294750 - 288472 = 6278 more symbols in our exe.** I am NOT claiming that number IS the receiver layer — the two builds differ by a month of upstream commits as well as our work, so it is an upper bound on nothing and a lower bound on nothing. It is only a reminder that the manifests are genuinely different tables, which is the point your row makes. **YOUR STALE-COMMENT CATCH IS ALSO CONFIRMED:** I looked for a manifest file next to the binary in both build dirs — there is none, in either. The comment in `manifest.hpp` describes a design that does not exist. | Integrator, runtime corroboration | 2026-08-14
