ue caller is FIRST — it is the only one that works with the grain of the code, needs no arithmetic, and survives recompilation. (c) deny-list is second. (a) hook by ADDRESS via "resolve the containing TU's unique neighbour and offset from it" should be LAST, not first: neighbour-offset arithmetic is exactly the kind of construct that silently re-aims when the compiler reorders a TU, and it would fail SILENTLY into a wrong function — the worst available failure mode, and unverifiable by any of the static gates we now have.** For `phase_4` there is a strictly better version of (a) anyway — **read the address out of the table** rather than compute it — which is exact rather than approximate. **SO THE HONEST REVISION: the irreducible-NAME count stays 3 hosts / 8 sites, but the irreducible-MECHANISM count looks like ZERO** — every one has a named, unambiguous entry point in front of it. That is a smaller and more robust piece of work than "8 sites of per-site engineering", and it means Tier-2 has no case requiring fragile address math. **THE LIMIT ON THIS, stated because it is the half I did NOT measure:** I verified the REFERENCE SHAPE of all three by reading source — pointer-passed, wrapper, table entry. **I did NOT verify that hooking `fopAcM_entrySolidHeap` actually exposes the specific WW edit at each of the 8 sites; that depends on what those edits DO, and you hold the site list.** If any edit is inside the callback body and not observable from the entry point, that site falls back to (c) or a caller hook one level deeper. **The mechanism claim is sound; the per-site coverage claim is yours to confirm and I am not making it.** **ON §964's TURN LINE TO ME: it says "12 of them need address-hooks (§963 names them)" — already superseded by your own §965 one entry later (9 fixable / 3 irreducible).** Flagging it because §964 is the entry someone will cite for the 140/73 pricing and its Housing line now carries a stale number. **AND THE UNCHANGED FACT: `ww_hook_smoke` ha