y] install` with both hooks `MOD_OK` and `rows:0` and nothing else changing — which makes it cheap to verify and impossible to blame for a regression. **TWO PLACES I DIVERGED FROM THE §968 WORDING, both deliberate and both reasoned in the source banner:** **(1) `cDyl_IsLinked` returns TRUE, not "not linked"** — per §969 and my row 202, arrived at independently from both ends: the function's own shape is `d = DMC[i]; if (d != NULL) rt = d->isLinked(); else rt = TRUE;` so "no module control" ALREADY means "treat as linked", and from the consumer side a FALSE short-circuits `fpcLd_Use` -> `fpcFCtRq_Request` returns NULL -> **every WW actor silently fails to spawn, no crash and no log.** **(2) The range test is against the PLUGIN'S OWN base index (4096), not `>= fpcNm_MAX_NUM`.** A plugin cannot see that constant without a receiver header, and hardcoding today's 829 would mis-fire on any dusklight whose proc list grew. Testing our own base is version-independent and strictly safer — everything below it falls through untouched whatever the host's real array size is. **ONE HAZARD RECORDED IN THE SOURCE FOR WHOEVER ADDS THE FIRST ACTOR ROW, because it is a silent-failure class and not a compile error: `fpcPf_Get` returns a `process_profile_definition*`, and this plugin carries NO receiver headers by design — that is exactly what makes it version-independent. A row must supply a LAYOUT-COMPATIBLE struct authored plugin-side, and a mismatch is a field-misread, not a build break. Derive the layout from the receiver and state the derivation in the CALLS row that adds it. Whatever is returned must also OUTLIVE THE CALL — plugin-owned static or table storage, never a stack temporary.** **AND THE TWO HOOKS OF THE §747 CHAIN HAVE OPPOSITE NULL CONTRACTS, which is worth every lane knowing before anyone writes the third: NULL is the correct "not mine" signal on `fpcPf_Get` (`fpcBs_Create`, f_pc_base.cpp:138, treats it as graceful creation failure) and is WRONG on `dStage_searchName`, whose consumer asserts non-NULL — there you answer for ours and FALL THROUGH.** `dStage_getName` is OFF the hook list entirely (no consumer branches on it). | user ruling via Housing; tale §968/§969, rows 202/217/219 | 2026-08-14 || HISTORY+BRIDGE ACK (tale §974), box left for other lanes. **BOTH YOUR DIVERGENCES FROM §968 CORRECT MY §949 AND YOU ARE RIGHT ON BOTH.** (1) Return value: I recommended 'not linked' for out-of-range; verified your reasoning from both ends — fpcLd_Use requires IsLoaded==TRUE, and fpcFCtRq_Request returns NULL on failure, so FALSE = every WW actor silently fails to spawn, no crash no log; plus the native else-branch already returns TRUE for a NULL entry. My value would have shipped the exact silent-failure class I have been warning other lanes about all day. (2) Range test against the plugin's own base (4096) rather than fpcNm_MAX_NUM is version-independent where mine was not — and version-independence is the product premise. **AND MY GAP-HUNT CONFIRMS YOUR VEHICLE INSTEAD OF BREAKING IT:** §949 named THREE cDyl_ accessors; the 2-hook count made me suspect despawn was uncovered. Source says otherwise — cDyl_Unlink is `#if TARGET_PC return FALSE;` and cDyl_LinkASync is `#if TARGET_PC return cPhs_COMPLEATE_e;`, so NEITHER touches DMC on PC. cDyl_IsLinked has NO TARGET_PC guard and is the ONLY live DMC read on this platform — exactly the one you hooked. **Two hooks is the correct minimal set, not an omission; my 'three accessors' was over-broad.** §949 should be read as superseded by §974. -> **INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED AT SOURCE BY HISTORY+BRIDGE (tale §974) — recorded here so a lane building on this ruling sees it was CHECKED, not merely asserted by its author.** They set out to find a gap in the 2-hook design and found the opposite. **THE COMPLETENESS RESULT, from their read of all three accessors on the PC path:** `cDyl_Unlink` -> `#if TARGET_PC return FALSE;` never touches `DMC`; `cDyl_LinkASync` -> `#if TARGET_PC return cPhs_COMPLEATE_e;` never touches `DMC`; **`cDyl_IsLinked` has NO `TARGET_PC` guard and is the only one of the three that actually reads `DMC[i_ProfName]` on this platform.** Their conclusion, in their words: *"the live OOB surface on PC is exactly ONE function, and it is precisely the one they hooked. Two hooks is not an omission, it is the correct minimal set."* **That matters more than agreement: it means the vehicle is minimal AND complete rather than merely sufficient, and a future lane does not need to wonder whether a third hook was forgotten.** **THEY ALSO CORRECTED THEIR OWN §949 ON THE RECORD, and it supersedes a finding still in circulation: §949 recommended bounds-checking "the three `cDyl_*` accessors" and returning the NOT-LINKED answer for out-of-range. Both halves were wrong — the extent was one accessor, not three, and the value was inverted.** Anyone citing §949's phrasing should read §974 instead. **I am not claiming a win off this.** Their §949 named the hazard in the first place, and my row 110 narrowed it 3->1 only because their row put `cDyl_` in front of me; the return-value correction was reached in the same pass by §969 and by my row 202 independently. **Three lanes converged on the same two facts by three different routes — consumer trace, callee semantics, and accessor sweep — which is why I would now describe the hook set as provably right rather than carefully chosen.** | Housing, recording §974's verification | 2026-08-14 -> **GATED AND BUILT. `ww_registry.dusk` 6,778 bytes, link clean — and the user's exe was NOT disturbed this time.**
