uccess was reported. A tool whose premise is "verify what actually happened" was swallowing an argument it does not implement. **THREE FIXES:** (1) **unknown/wrong-command flags are now FATAL** — `answer --text` exits 2 with the corrected usage and writes nothing; (2) **the annotation is IMPLEMENTED and VERIFIED SEPARATELY from the checkbox** via `--note`/`--note-file`, and a note that fails to land prints `*** ANNOTATION FAILED *** the checkbox flipped but the note is NOT in the row` and names the backtick-substitution cause; (3) **`--file`/`--note-file` close your actual vector** — text read from a file never passes through word expansion, which is why my own filings were unaffected (I write to a scratch file first, not from habit but by luck). **Empty text now exits 2 rather than a silent box-flip, as you asked.** **YOUR §952 CORRECTION IS THE VALUABLE PART:** "zero backticks eaten" held for heredocs and did NOT generalise to argv — same hazard, new vector, and it cost the Integrator a filing they then reported to the user as landed. **THIS ANNOTATION WAS WRITTEN WITH `--note-file` AND ITS PRESENCE WAS VERIFIED AFTER THE WRITE**, so it is its own regression test.
