THE DATA HAS BEEN SITTING UNREAD. WwProbe903 is in the tree and HAS been firing — logs 011107 and 012809 both carry it. Nobody looked, me included. The 012809 line: UNITRANS styleTimer=0 transTimer=1008981770 TimerParam=1 paramVal=20 type=1.

WHAT IT SETTLES — H2 IS DEAD BY MEASUREMENT: TimerParam=1, paramVal=20. The "Timer" event parameter resolves with value 20, so the early-out "no Timer, end the cut immediately" is disproved. Not argued — measured.

WHAT IT DOES NOT SETTLE, AND WHY THAT IS MY FAULT: transTimer=1008981770 looks like a smoking gun — as a float bit pattern it is 0x3C23D70A = 0.01f, an int field holding a float. I nearly filed it as the mechanism. It is not evidence of anything. My probe sits at line 971, BEFORE the "if (mCurCamStyleTimer == 0)" init block that populates trans->mTimer. TransData is a view onto mWork[0x100], a scratch buffer reused across camera cut types, so a pre-init read is EXPECTED to hold another cut's leftovers — 0.01f is somebody's Rate or Cushion. Sampling before initialisation makes the field meaningless by construction, and reporting it would have been the same narrow-surface error that has caught six lanes today.

LIVE HYPOTHESIS SET, for your routing: H2 no-Timer = DEAD. H1 re-entry (mCurCamStyleTimer re-zeroed each frame so the cut re-inits forever), H3 nonsense mTimer post-init, H4 counting-but-never-arriving = ALL STILL OPEN. The completion gate is mCurCamStyleTimer >= trans->mTimer, so the discriminator is those two values sampled AFTER init on consecutive frames.

THE FIX IS PROBE PLACEMENT, NOT RESEARCH: move the sample below the init block and log both values each firing. Rising styleTimer against a sane mTimer is H4; styleTimer stuck at 0 is H1; wild mTimer post-init is H3. I need your gate word to land it — it edits a native TP file, and while you rule an existing seam is not a new one, clause (2) of §939 is the user's rule and I will not read it generously in my own favour.

