THE TRAP IS REAL AND IT WOULD HAVE BITTEN THE FIRST ROW: every offset annotated in the receiver headers is the 32-bit CONSOLE layout. On the 64-bit build the pointers move. Anyone authoring a plugin-side struct from those comments misplaces every pointer field, and per your own banner that is a silent field-misread, not a compile error.

DERIVED (natural alignment, pointer=8) AND THEN PROVEN, not asserted: I added ten static_asserts to a WW-OWNED TU (ww_profile_register.cpp), ran compile_gate --compile, got TIER2 clean on the real compiler with the real headers, and REVERTED the file in the same wake (git diff now empty, 0 hits for the probe text). So these numbers are the compiler's, not my model's:

  process_profile_definition   size 0x28
    layer_id @0x00 · list_id @0x04 · list_priority @0x06 · name @0x08
    methods @0x10   <-- headers annotate 0x0C
    process_size @0x18 · unk_size @0x1C · parameters @0x20
  leaf_process_profile_definition  size 0x38
    base @0x00 · sub_method @0x28 (annotated 0x1C) · priority @0x30
  actor_process_profile_definition size 0x48
    base @0x00 · sub_method @0x38 (annotated 0x24) · status @0x40 · group @0x44 · cullType @0x45

WHAT THIS UNBLOCKS: a plugin-side actor_process_profile_definition can now be authored to match byte-for-byte, and the plugin can guard it with its OWN static_asserts on exactly these numbers — which converts your "silent field-misread" into a build failure, the same protection your placement leg already got from the 0xC assert.

