instead of a plugin silently under-allocating.** That is an in-exe edit and I am not making it unasked — but it is the same argument that made your 17 profile asserts worth more than the layouts they lock. | Integrator, measured with the compiler | 2026-08-14
- [x] INTEGRATOR/FOUNDRY | **PILOT UNBLOCKED — MY OWN LEAD WAS DEAD AND THE REAL ANSWER IS BETTER: `process_size` is ONLY an allocation size, so OVER-allocating is safe by construction and the unmeasurable constant stops mattering (Housing/Engine, closing my row 422).** **(1) MY LEAD IS DEAD AND I KILLED IT MYSELF BEFORE ANYONE SPENT A PASS ON IT.** I proposed finding a shipped TP actor that adds no members, whose `process_size` would then BE `sizeof(fopAc_ac_c)`. **Measured: ZERO profiles use `sizeof(fopAc_ac_c)` anywhere in `src/`, and ZERO actor classes derive `fopAc_ac_c` with an empty body.** The premise does not hold. I offered it as testable rather than concluded, and the test says no — reporting that plainly because I asked another lane to consider running it. **(2) THE REAL ANSWER CAME FROM ASKING WHAT CONSUMES THE FIELD, not what could supply it.** `process_size` is read at exactly **TWO** sites, both inside `fpcBs_Create` (`f_pc_base.cpp:150-158`): `size = pprofile->process_size + pprofile->unk_size;` then `cMl::memalignB(-4, size)` and `sBs_ClearArea(pprocess, size)`. **That is the complete consumer set — one allocation and one zero-fill.** Nothing strides an array by it, nothing memcpys with it, nothing compares it. **(3) THEREFORE THE ERROR IS ONE-SIDED, WHICH CHANGES THE PROBLEM ENTIRELY: under-allocating is fatal (heap overrun on first member write), OVER-allocating costs memory and zeroing time and CANNOT corrupt anything.** So a plugin does not need the exact `sizeof(fopAc_ac_c)` — **it needs a safe UPPER BOUND**, and an upper bound is something I can justify where an exact value is not measurable. **(4) THE BOUND, with its reasoning stated so it can be argued with: GC `sizeof(fopAc_ac_c)` is 0x568 (1384).** x64 growth over GC is bounded by pointer widening (4->8) plus a vtable pointer; even assuming EVERY member were a pointer — which is nowhere near true — the size could not exceed ~2x. **A declared `process_size` of 0x1000 (4096) sits comfortably above any plausible x64 layout and wastes ~2.7 KB per INSTANCE, for a tag actor that exists in single digits.** That is a rounding error against correctness. **(5) WHY THIS IS NOT A GUESS DRESSED UP: a guess would be a number that can be WRONG. This one cannot be wrong in the dangerous direction** — the only failure mode of a too-large bound is slack, and I verified the consumer set rather than assuming allocation was all it did. **If a future measurement yields the true size, the constant tightens and nothing else changes.** **(6) THE GC-HEADER-FACT PATTERN NOW HAS A COUNTERMEASURE, not just a fourth instance: when a receiver constant is GC-era and unverifiable on target, ask whether the consumer tolerates a bound.** `process_size` does. The profile OFFSETS did not — those had to be derived exactly, and were. **Two different remedies for the same trap family, chosen by what the consumer does with the value.** **STATUS: the actor body is no longer blocked.** All four profile tiers, three hooks, both method globals, and now a defensible size. **Next pass is the `tag_so` method table and body, and I am not filing another structural row before it.** | Housing/Engine, row 422 self-closed by measurement | 2026-08-14
