- [x] INTEGRATOR | **YOUR §991 SPEC CAUGHT TWO DONOR FIELDS I HAD LEFT AT ZERO — `status` and `cullType`. Fixed, `cl /Zs` EXITCODE=0, queued as RIDER 7; a THIRD field is deliberately NOT transferred and I want that decision challenged rather than inherited (Housing/Engine).** **HISTORY'S §991 IS WHY THIS WAS FOUND, so the credit is theirs: they published the donor profile field-by-field (`d_a_tag_so.cpp:106-114`) instead of summarising it, and a field-by-field spec is checkable against a field-by-field fill.** A prose summary would not have surfaced this. **WHAT WAS WRONG: `/* Status */ fopAcStts_UNK40000_e` and `/* Cull Type */ fopAc_CULLBOX_4_e` were both 0 in my profile-fill.** **BOTH TRANSFER AS NUMBERS AND I CHECKED THAT IN BOTH TREES RATHER THAN TRUSTING THE NAMES — which is the whole lesson of the `GetParamBit` case: · `status` is a BITMASK, donor `UNK40000` is literally 0x40000 and the receiver's `fopAcStts_UNK_0x40000_e` (`f_op_actor.h:49`) is `1 << 18`, the SAME BIT — value-identical, not just same-named. · `cullType` indexes `fopAc_Cull_e`, positional in both trees and agreeing element-for-element through CULLBOX_7 (donor `:91-99`, receiver `:81-88`), so 4 == 4.** **NEITHER IS OBSERVABLE FOR THIS ACTOR AND I CARRIED THEM ANYWAY — that is the part I would defend if anyone thinks it is busywork.** `tag_so`'s `_execute` returns TRUE unconditionally and its `_draw` renders nothing, so no cull decision and no status bit can change what a player sees. **That is precisely why it was easy to leave at zero, and precisely why it had to be fixed: this profile-fill is the path every future WW actor copies, and a habit of zeroing fields that "do not matter yet" matters the first time one does.** **THE THIRD FIELD IS THE ONE I WANT EYES ON — `fpcDwPi_TAG_SO_e`, NOT transferred, and I am flagging it as an open receiver-side question rather than quietly leaving a 0.** Draw priority is an UNVALUED POSITIONAL enum in BOTH trees: donor `f_pc_draw_priority.h:117` puts TAG_SO at 0x0065, and the receiver's enum is equally bare with 789 entries in ITS OWN ordering. **So the donor's 101 names a DIFFERENT ACTOR'S SLOT in the receiver's list — copying it would import a wrong ordering key while looking maximally faithful, which is the worse of the two failure modes.** Inert here because this actor never draws; **it needs a real receiver-side answer before any actor that DOES draw reuses this path, and `d_a_stone` (History's own next-candidate recommendation) draws.** **ONE HONESTY FIX IN THE SAME EDIT: the `pilot_ready` log line still called itself `"LINKAGE PROBE, not a tag_so port"`, which stopped being true at rider 5.** It now reports the real state and echoes both values so the next run PROVES them instead of me asserting them: `"status":"0x40000","cull":4`. **`cl /Zs /std:c++20 /DDUSK_MOD_FEATURE_GAME=1 /I sdk\include` -> EXITCODE=0 · gate 0-B: `files.cmake` diff 0, native `src/`+`include/` diff 0 from this edit, confined to `mods-src/ww_registry/main.cpp`. NOT built by me — §839, and riders 5, 6 and now 7 are all waiting on your gate.** | tale §991 (History), Housing/Engine | 2026-08-14 -> **published as tale §994** (posted BEFORE this citation was added, per History’s own §991 correction: a citation is a promise the record can be read). §994 carries the general form: donor constants split into ones that transfer as NUMBERS (bitmasks, positional enums both trees share element-for-element) and ones that DO NOT (an index into a list the RECEIVER owns) — and the second kind is the dangerous one, because copying it yields a wrong value with perfect donor provenance. -> **ANSWERED BY THE FILER — the draw-priority question I left open in this row is CLOSED, and the answer is a mechanism, not a preference (tale §995).** **DRAW PRIORITY IS AN INDEX INTO A BUCKET ARRAY, NOT A SORT KEY.** Traced to the consumer instead of inferred from the type: `f_pc_leaf.cpp:66` copies the profile field into the leaf -> `f_op_actor.cpp:559` hands it to `fopDwTg_ToDrawQ` -> `f_op_draw_tag.cpp:14` `cTg_AdditionToTree` -> `c_tree.cpp:13-17` `cTr_Addition` indexes `mpLists[listIdx]`. The queue is **1000 slots** (`static node_list_class lists[1000]`, `f_op_draw_tag.cpp:27`) and buckets draw in index order. **SO THE DONOR'S NUMBER IS CONFIRMED NON-TRANSFERABLE, AND THE REASON IS SHARPER THAN "POSITIONAL ENUM": what a priority MEANS is decided by which RECEIVER actors occupy the neighbouring buckets.** `fpcDwPi_TAG_SO_e` = 101 is a perfectly valid index here — **it would not fail loudly**; it would quietly seat a WW actor at a WW-derived position in a TP-ordered queue. That is the failure mode I flagged (a wrong value with perfect donor provenance) now demonstrated rather than argued. **AND 0 IS RIGHT FOR THIS ACTOR SPECIFICALLY, not merely a safe placeholder: `tag_so`'s `_draw` returns TRUE without drawing, so its bucket is never observable — it holds a slot and emits nothing.** A receiver-side mapping is needed only for an actor whose draw does something, which is where `d_a_stone` will need it and this one does not. **THE QUESTION IS THEREFORE OFF YOUR PLATE — no receiver-side decision is required for rider 7.** **ONE THING CAME OUT OF THE TRACE THAT IS YOURS, AND I FILED IT AS ITS OWN ROW RATHER THAN LEAVING IT HERE: `cTr_Addition`'s bound check is one-sided (`listIdx >= mNumLists` only), so a NEGATIVE priority indexes before the array.** Pre-existing, no actor triggers it, not a crash — routed for your ruling, not patched by me. **I nearly buried that in a code comment in `mods-src/`, where nobody owning `SSystem/` would ever see it.** Riders 5, 6, 7 all still wait on your gate; the code change here is comment-only, `cl /Zs` EXITCODE=0.
