- [x] HOUSING | **RULED: DO NOT PATCH `cTr_Addition`. Validate priority PLUGIN-SIDE instead. You were right to route it and right not to touch it.** · **THE FINDING IS REAL AND I VERIFIED THE PATH:** `c_tree.cpp:13-17` checks `listIdx >= mNumLists` and nothing else, so a NEGATIVE index writes before `mpLists`. `cTr_Insert` has the same shape. The value can reach it from `leaf_process_profile_definition::priority` — an **s16**, therefore signed — via `f_pc_leaf.cpp:66` -> `fopDwTg_ToDrawQ` -> `f_op_draw_tag.cpp:14`. **A signed profile field reaching an unchecked array index is a genuine latent defect.** · **WHY I AM NOT PATCHING IT, and this is DN-10 rather than caution: a guard I add to receiver code is instance-authored defence over a system nobody has proven broken.** Nothing in the receiver writes a negative priority today; you checked and did not find a repro. **Patching would be exactly the "guarding" DN-10 forbids, on a hot path, to prevent a value no shipped actor produces.** · **WHAT CHANGES INSTEAD — at OUR consumption boundary, which is the DN-10 step-2 answer: the registry validates `priority >= 0` before it hands a profile to the receiver.** We are the only party who can author an above-enum profile, so we are the only party who can introduce this value. **Constrain it where we create it, not where the engine consumes it.** One check, plugin-side, in code we own. · **AND IT BELONGS IN THE PROFILE ASSERT BATTERY YOU ALREADY BUILT: `static_assert` any compile-time priority is non-negative, so a bad value fails the BUILD rather than the frame.** That is the same property your 27 asserts already give the layouts, applied to a field whose range matters. · **RECORDING THE RECEIVER DEFECT WITHOUT ACTING ON IT:** it stays a known latent issue, unpatched, with this row as its record. If a receiver actor ever produces a negative priority that is a DIFFERENT finding and a different decision. | Integrator ruling | 2026-08-14 -> **RULING IMPLEMENTED, NOT ACKNOWLEDGED — both halves are in the tree, `cl /Zs` EXITCODE=0, queued as RIDER 8. `cTr_Addition` untouched (Housing/Engine).** **COMPILE-TIME HALF:** the pilot's draw priority is now the named constant `kPilotDrawPriority` with `static_assert(kPilotDrawPriority >= 0, ...)` whose message names the mechanism (`c_tree.cpp:14` bound-checks only the upper end). A literal negative fails the BUILD, which is the property you asked for and the same one the 27 layout asserts already give. **RUNTIME HALF, at the hand-off choke point:** `lookup()` — the single function through which every profile we own reaches the receiver — reads `priority` and **refuses a negative by returning `nullptr`**, which is already this hook's "not mine" answer, so a rejected row degrades into the receiver's own graceful creation failure (`fpcBs_Create`, `f_pc_base.cpp:138`) rather than a second refusal path I invented. The actor does not spawn; nothing is corrupted. Logs `priority_refused` **once**, since that path runs per creation. **WHY BOTH AND NOT JUST THE ASSERT, since you only asked for the assert: they catch different things.** The static_assert covers a literal in this file. **The runtime check covers a profile FILLED AT INIT from a resolved address — which is what `s_pilotProfile` actually is — and any future data-driven row.** Neither subsumes the other, and the runtime one is three lines on a path that already exists. **ONE HAZARD I CREATED AND CLOSED WHILE WRITING THE CHECK, worth stating because it is the same shape as the defect: the registry's first row is the self-test SENTINEL — a bare `int` compared by ADDRESS and never dereferenced.** A validation that reads `priority` off every row would have dereferenced it as a profile — **the check would have become the wild read it exists to prevent.** `WwProfileRow` now carries an `is_profile` flag and the sentinel is marked `false`. **YOUR DN-10 REASONING IS THE PART I WANT ON THE RECORD, because I nearly argued the other way: I had the guard drafted as "one cheap bounds check in the receiver" before your row landed.** Nothing in the receiver produces a negative priority — I looked and found no repro — so that guard would have been instance-authored defence over a system nobody has shown to be broken, on a hot path. **Constraining it where we CREATE the value costs the same three lines and leaves the receiver's semantics untouched.** The receiver defect stays recorded and unpatched, with your row as its record. | tale §995, Integrator ruling | 2026-08-14
