- [ ] INTEGRATOR, HOUSING | **MEASURED: THE WARP UI IS NOT CLOSED — the DATA path is shut but the CODE path is open on stock dusklight. And an anonymous-namespace finding that widens what a plugin can reach in general (Foundry).** · **YOUR QUESTION, verbatim: _"can a plugin ADD a warp destination, or is the warp UI closed?"_ Three-part answer, all measured. · **(1) THE DATA PATH IS CLOSED, and definitively.** Destinations come from `static const auto gameRegions = std::to_array({...})` in `include/dusk/map_loader_definitions.h` — a **compile-time table with internal linkage, declared in a HEADER**, so every TU gets its own const copy. **A plugin cannot add a destination by data. There is no table to append to.** · **(2) THE CODE PATH IS OPEN — and this is the part I did not expect.** `populate_map_picker`, `selected_region`, `clamp_indices` and `can_warp` are all in an **ANONYMOUS NAMESPACE** — internal linkage, invisible to any export table — **yet all four appear in the symbol manifest and resolve UNIQUELY on VANILLA upstream** as `dusk::ui::`anonymous namespace'::populate_map_picker` and siblings. **So the warp UI's population path is hookable by name on a stock exe.** A plugin injects entries at UI-BUILD time rather than at data time. · **(3) THE FINDING THAT GENERALISES BEYOND WARP, and it should change how every lane scopes plugin reach: the symgen manifest carries ANONYMOUS-NAMESPACE symbols.** Every previous estimate of "what a plugin can hook" implicitly assumed external linkage. **It does not bind.** Far more of the fork is reachable than an exports-based view suggests — this is worth re-checking before anyone declares another surface closed. · **THE CONTROL THAT MAKES THIS CREDIBLE RATHER THAN A HOPEFUL READ: the same query returns MISSING on vanilla for `setRoomLayerOverride` and `markDebugWarpStorySuppress`** — both OUR fork's WW additions. **The tool distinguishes present-from-absent on the same image in the same pass**, so the four SAFE verdicts are n