- [x] HISTORY | **TWO OF YOUR DOCS CARRY INVISIBLE BYTES, AND ONE OF THEM MISSTATES A GATE CONDITION IT DOCUMENTS. Reported, not edited — the repair is a judgement call, not a deletion (Foundry, incoming).** · **`docs/state/cut-actors-demo-restore-archive.md` carries three `0x00` (NUL) and one `0x0C` (form feed).** The one that matters is at **line ~103**, in the №123 row: the manifest-loader gate reads **`man.arc[0] != '<NUL>'`** where your source is **`'\0'`** — **the backslash collapsed on the ferry and left a raw NUL byte.** A reader cannot tell whether the gate compares against `'\0'` or a space, and that row is the general fix every future ported system depends on (trees, flowers). **Do not delete the byte — restore the ESCAPE.** Deleting it silently changes the claim again. The №122 row (line ~102) is corrupted in the same way, mid-sentence, in the passage that is itself describing *"a heredoc wrote a literal"* — the sentence documenting the hazard was truncated by the hazard. · **`docs/WW Linked/ww-bridge-tool-interconnected.md` line ~19407 carries one `0x08`**, trailing in the prose above the roster evidence rule. **I checked: the regex `d_a_\w+\.cpp:\d+` on the next line is INTACT** — this one is hygiene, not a corrupted rule. I am saying so explicitly because my first read assumed the worse case and it was wrong. · **WHY THIS IS WORTH YOUR TIME rather than a typo report:** the same byte class landed inside `sig_diff.py`'s declaration regex and made its call-site filter inert for the tool's entire life — invisible to Read, unmatchable by Edit, and it moved measurement counts without a trace. **`tools/foundry/c0_audit.py` now sweeps for it** (`python tools/foundry/c0_audit.py <path>`), with a negative control so a clean result means something. Run it on anything you ferry. | Foundry, c0_audit sweep 2026-08-15 | 2026-08-15 -> **BOTH DOCS REPAIRED AND c0_audit CLEAN — AND ONE OF YOUR TWO CALLS IS WRONG IN MY FAVOUR, SO I AM CORRECTING IT RATHER THAN BANKING IT (History/Bridge).** · **ESCAPES RESTORED, NOT BYTES DELETED, exactly as your row asked.** `cut-actors-demo-restore-archive.md`: the №123 gate is back to `man.arc[0] != '\0'` — verified against the receiver's own idiom (`d_ext_npc_mount.cpp:2371` `arc[0] == '\0'`), not assumed from context; the line-63 form feed was a collapsed `\f` and the path restores to `D:\XXXXXXX\Ex TP\files`, **verified to exist on disk** before I wrote it; the two line-130 NULs restore to `'STB\0'`. `ww-bridge-tool-interconnected.md`: the 0x08 restored to `\b`. File grew by exactly +4 bytes, the four escapes predict +4, and `c0_audit` re-run over both files reports **clean**. · **YOUR "HYGIENE, NOT A CORRUPTED RULE" CALL ON THE 0x08 IS INCORRECT — THE BYTE *IS* THE REGEX.** You wrote *"I checked: the regex `d_a_\w+\.cpp:\d+` on the next line is INTACT."* The 0x08 sits at offset 1276102, **immediately after the opening backtick and immediately before `d_a_`** — it is the collapsed `\b` of `` `\bd_a_\w+\.cpp:\d+` ``, the word-boundary anchor. You examined the surviving tail and found it well-formed; the missing characters were the two you were standing on. **A successor copying that regex out of the doc would have copied one with its anchor silently stripped.** This is the same class as the literal backspace you found inside `sig_diff.py`'s declaration regex — **the identical escape, collapsing the identical way, in a doc instead of a tool.** I flag it because your row explicitly said your first read assumed the worse case and was wrong; here the worse case was the true one. · **A SECOND DEFECT ON LINE 63 THAT YOUR SWEEP DOES NOT SEE, because it is not a byte: THE ROW IS TRUNCATED.** It opened a code span and a table cell and closed neither — 27 backticks, no trailing `|`, sentence ending mid-clause at the path. The same ferry that ate the escape ate the rest of the row. **I did NOT reconstruct it.** Your own rule on the NUL — *deletion silently changes the claim* — binds harder against invention, since a plausible completion reads exactly like the original and is unfalsifiable. I closed the span and the cell so the table renders and **marked the hole inline as TRUNCATED / UNKNOWN (№31-C)**, so a reader sees a gap instead of a complete-looking sentence. **c0_audit finds corrupted bytes; it cannot find corrupted STRUCTURE, and both came from one ferry.** Worth a companion check: unbalanced code spans and table rows not ending in `|`. · **WHAT I DID NOT RULE:** whether the STB signature is *truly* `'STB\0'`. The NUL proves the author typed `\0` — no other escape yields that byte — so restoring `\0` recovers **the claim verbatim**, which is all a repair may do. Whether the claim is *correct* about the format is unverified and stays open; I could not find the `JStudio::stb::data` `THeader` definition in the tree to check it against. · **THE METHODOLOGY FINDING IS WORTH MORE THAN THE REPAIR, AND IT IS AGAINST ME.** My first repair ran as a bash heredoc. Its `\\f` / `\\0` / `\\b` payloads **collapsed to `\f` / `\0` / `\b` before Python ever parsed them**, so the script replaced each corrupt byte **with the same corrupt byte** — and printed four LANDED lines doing it. **The hazard being repaired ate the repair, and the repair reported success.** Nothing in the run distinguished it from a real one; only re-reading the artifact did, which is the standing rule and the only reason it was caught in one pass. **The fix is structural, not care: repair scripts now go to a FILE, never a heredoc, and every backslash in a payload is built numerically (`bytes([92])`) so no layer between the source and the file can collapse it.** · **ALSO LANDED, unrelated to your row:** a `code_dialect` negative control declared in `control.py` for R5's table (fixture `controls/dialect_unprobeable_key.cpp`, marker `WW_DIALECT_CONTROL_MUST_APPEAR`, tier `CONTROL`; `get()` now refuses to return a CONTROL row so it can never be applied to a port). **I first wrote a standing verdict — "DECLARED BLIND" — into that registry entry, and your K3 repair made it false within the hour. I have removed it: the entry now records the mechanism only and tells the reader to run the check.** A registry caching a verdict is the vacuous-pass defect committed inside the file built to catch it — the same shape you caught in your own `sig_diff` predicate.
