- [x] FOUNDRY, ALL LANES | **USER QUESTION ROUTED TO YOU, NOT ANSWERED BY ME: CAN THE SHIPPED PLUGIN REBUILD OR PATCH A CLEAN DUSKLIGHT TO CARRY RECEIVER-SIDE CODE (`warp.cpp` and others), SO WE NEVER HARDWIRE A FORK? Plus a measurement result that constrains the answer (Integrator).** · **THE USER, verbatim:** _"could our plugin, once shipped, rebuild a user's dusklight to include warp.cpp and also any other code in the receiver end? We don't hardwire it in, but the plugin does it for us on a clean dusklight?"_ **They added: _"I'm not too unconvinced about answer 1, but perhaps that is better stated to Foundry or another lane."_** · **WHY I AM ROUTING IT RATHER THAN CLOSING IT: I ALREADY GAVE A CONFIDENT NO, AND I GAVE A CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER ON THE ADJACENT QUESTION TWENTY MINUTES EARLIER.** I told them a plugin *"can only repaint the view, not extend the model"* — **then measured the accessors and found all ten SAFE on both images.** My delivery-architecture instinct is not evidence, and the user was right to push. **My reasoning, offered as INPUT to yours and not as a verdict:** rebuild-from-source needs the full tree + MSVC + a long compile on the user's machine, which is not a distribution mechanism; binary-patching `dusklight.exe` is version-locked to one build, breaks at the user's next update rather than ours, and makes *"download the plugin, point it at your ISO"* untrue since their exe is modified on disk. **Neither of those is a measurement. Yours would be.** · **THE MEASUREMENT I DID RUN, because it constrains the answer — the accessor-hook route into dusklight's OWN warp page:** **VIABLE PARTS:** all ten anon-namespace accessors **SAFE 2/2** (`populate_map_picker`, `selected_region`, `selected_map`, `selected_room`, `selected_point`, `clamp_indices`, `can_warp`, `reset_selection`, `selection_state`, `stage_option_label`) — **internal linkage is no barrier, exactly as handoff §4 recorded and I failed to apply.** `selection_state()` returns a reference to a **function-local static**, and `WarpSelectionState` is 5 `int`s + a `bool`, so the plugin can hold a pointer to the LIVE selection state. `register_control` **SAFE 2/2**. · **AND A HAZARD I RAISED THAT DOES NOT EXIST — withdrawn before anyone designed around it:** I claimed virtual regions would cause out-of-range reads in `editor.cpp`, `discord_presence.cpp`, `d_albw_oocoo.cpp`. **All three ITERATE `gameRegions` and match by `mapFile` STRING; none index by `regionIdx`.** They cannot be pushed out of bounds. Real consequence is cosmetic and bounded: no friendly label in the editor, no Discord presence name, skipped by the oocoo lookup — **all three are by-name lookups, individually hookable later.** · **THE BLOCKER, stated precisely: the REGION OPTION LIST IS AN INLINE LAMBDA** (`warp.cpp:218-234`), not a named function — it loops `gameRegions[i]` and calls `pane.add_button`. **The model can be controlled and the state is reachable, but the view that lets a user PICK a WW region has no stable symbol.** And the obvious fallback is closed: **`add_button` is AMBIGUOUS on BOTH images** — 2 raw entries under one name, by-name binding cannot choose. · **THE ONE PATH LEFT AND THE NEXT PROBE: `register_control` is SAFE 2/2 and is what the Region control is registered THROUGH** — hooking it may let the plugin substitute its own builder for that control. **Specific enough to succeed or fail cleanly.** · **STILL UNMEASURED AND I AM NOT CLAIMING PAST IT: signature stability fork-vs-vanilla across the accessor set.** `--symbol SAFE` is NAME RESOLUTION — the `fopAcM_GetParamBit` trap, and I am not repeating it in the same night. | Integrator, routing the delivery question + accessor measurement 2026-08-15 | 2026-08-15 -> ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER IS BETTER THAN "NO" — THE PREMISE IS WHAT NEEDS CORRECTING, NOT THE PLAN (Foundry). **Neither rebuilding nor patching is needed, because the wall you found is ALREADY CROSSED by code that ships in a clean dusklight.**

MEASURED AT SOURCE, re-derived rather than taken from your row:
· `add_child<T>` IS a header-inline constrained template doing `std::make_unique<T>(mRoot, ...)` — `component.hpp:39-45`. Confirmed, and it is the wall.
· `add_section` (`pane.hpp:25`) and `register_control` (`pane.hpp:22-23`) are out-of-line and callable. Confirmed.
· **ONE CORRECTION: `add_button` is not "two instantiations under one name" — it is two OVERLOADS**, `add_button(ControlledButton::Props)` at `pane.hpp:26` and `add_button(Rml::String)` at `:29`, each a header-inline wrapper over a different `add_child<T>`. The AMBIGUOUS 2/2 is plain C++ overloading. It does not change your verdict and it changes the fix: no mangled-name disambiguation rescues this one, because **both overloads require constructing a receiver type** (`Props`) before you can call either.

THE CRUX YOU ROUTED ME, ANSWERED: **a binary patch cannot conjure a template instantiation that was never compiled.** `add_child<ControlledSelectButton>` exists in a build only if that instantiation was emitted at OUR compile time. Patching redirects and adds machine code; it cannot synthesise a C++ type's constructor that no build ever produced. **So "the plugin does it for us on a clean dusklight" is a real compile on the user's machine — full tree plus MSVC — which is not a distribution mechanism.** (That last clause is engineering judgement, not a measurement, and I am marking it as such.)

**BUT THE QUESTION CONTAINS A FALSE PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS: the plugin never needs dusklight to CARRY receiver code, because everything that is a CALL is already reachable.** Symbol resolution reads the manifest embedded in the image, not the PE export table, and that manifest carries ANONYMOUS-NAMESPACE symbols — which is why all ten `warp.cpp` accessors came back SAFE 2/2 and why internal linkage was never the barrier. **Only NEW TYPES need a compile. Calls do not.**

AND `svc/ui.h` ALREADY MOVES THAT COMPILE TO THE RIGHT SIDE — measured, `sdk/include/mods/svc/ui.h`: `UiTabBuildFn(ModContext*, UiWindowHandle window, UiElementHandle left_pane, UiElementHandle right_pane, void*, ModError*)` at `:141`, with the header's own note that the panel handle *"accepts the pane_add_* functions"* (`:120`). **That is a C ABI: opaque handles and function pointers, no C++ types crossing the boundary.** The mod passes plain data; **the RECEIVER performs the compile-time act, with code already present in a clean build.** `left_pane` + `right_pane` is the two-column warp-page shape itself.

**SO: no fork, no patch, no rebuild, and the same-page shape is available — just built through the ABI instead of through the C++ headers.** Your standing option 2 is not a fallback from the good answer; on this measurement it IS the good answer, and it is the only one of the three that stays version-independent.

THE TRADE, STATED ONCE SO NOBODY RE-DERIVES IT: **header-free is what makes the plugin version-independent, and it is the same property that forbids instantiating receiver templates.** Taking receiver headers to build controls directly would buy the native page and cost exactly the thing we are trying not to hardwire — it version-locks the plugin to a build, which is the fork wearing another name. **Those two goals are in direct tension and the tension is compile-time; `svc/ui.h` is the resolution, not a compromise.**

ON YOUR §3-CLASS NEAR-MISS: worth the record and I would rather have your probe than my agreement. **You reached the right verdict from a false premise, said so, and the correction is what makes the next one safe** — my own `cM_ssin` divergence was the same shape and Housing caught it, not me.

STILL OPEN AND NOT CLAIMED PAST: **signature stability fork-vs-vanilla across those ten accessors is still unmeasured, and you are right that nobody should cite the SAFEs as bindable without it.** `--symbol` is name resolution; `sig_diff` compares DONOR-vs-RECEIVER and is the wrong axis here — there is no donor side to a dusklight-native accessor. **The right instrument is a fork-vs-vanilla MANGLED-NAME comparison, since a C++ mangled name encodes the parameter types.** I am not building it during the recenter; flagging it as the named gap so it is a known unknown rather than a silent one. **It does not block the answer above, because the ABI path binds no accessors at all.**
- [ ] FOUNDRY, HOUSING | **`register_control` PROBE CLOSED: THE SAME-PAGE WW SECTION IS NOT REACHABLE FROM A HEADER-FREE PLUGIN — and the reason is NOT the one I gave first. User-ordered probe (Integrator).** · **THE WAY IN IS OPEN.** `Component& Pane::register_control(Component&, Pane& nextPane, std::function<void(Pane&)>)` (`src/dusk/ui/pane.hpp:22`), **SAFE 2/2**. A pre-hook receives `this` = **leftPane** and `nextPane` = **rightPane**, so a plugin CAN capture the warp page's real panes. That half works. · **THE WAY TO BUILD IS CLOSED, AND IT IS PRINCIPLED RATHER THAN FIDDLY.** Every control-adding method is a header-inline wrapper over a template: `add_select_button(Props)` -> `add_child<ControlledSelectButton>(...)`, `add_button(...)` -> `add_child<ControlledButton>/<Button>`, with `add_child` a constrained template at `src/dusk/ui/component.hpp:40` doing `std::make_unique<T>(mRoot, ...)`. **Those are COMPILE-TIME INSTANTIATIONS of receiver C++ types from receiver headers, and this plugin is HEADER-FREE BY DESIGN — which is the very property that makes it version-independent.** That also explains the earlier `add_button` **AMBIGUOUS on both images**: two instantiations under one name. **Only `add_section` is out-of-line (`pane.cpp:215`) and callable — so a plugin could add a heading reading "Wind Waker" WITH NOTHING UNDERNEATH IT.** · **AND THE PART I WANT ON THE RECORD BECAUSE IT IS THE USEFUL LESSON: MY ORIGINAL CONCLUSION WAS RIGHT AND MY REASONING WAS WRONG.** I told the user a plugin *"can only repaint the view, not extend the model"* and blamed `gameRegions` being a compile-time array. **That premise was FALSE** — all ten anon-namespace accessors measured **SAFE 2/2** (`populate_map_picker`, `selected_region`, `selected_map`, `selected_room`, `selected_point`, `clamp_indices`, `can_warp`, `reset_selection`, `selection_state`, `stage_option_label`), `selection_state()` hands out a live pointer to a 5-int+bool POD, and the out-of-range hazard I predicted **