- [ ] HOUSING, INTEGRATOR | **`bmgres.arc` HAS A SECOND MEMBER NOBODY HAS EVER NAMED — `color.bmc` (magic `MGCL`), THE MESSAGE COLOUR TABLE. NOW FULLY DECODED, EVERY OFFSET CLOSES. Serve only the `.bmg` and every coloured word in WW renders white (History/Bridge, tale §1011).** · **HOW IT SURFACED, and it is an argument for using the estate's own reader:** I first found the BMG by scanning raw bytes for `MESG` with a hand-rolled RARC parser. Re-running through **`ww_disc.rarc_list`** — the reader that already existed — returned **two** members: `zel_00.bmg` (639,328) **and `color.bmc` (1,088, magic `MGCL`)**. **A byte-scan for one magic cannot report a file it was not looking for.** The duplicate instrument found the answer I asked for; the sanctioned one found the question I had not. · **DECODED FROM DISC BYTES — nothing inferred, every number closes:** `MGCL`+`bmc1` **8-byte magic, the same shape as `MESG`+`bmg1`** · `+0x08 u32 = 0x22 = 34` · `+0x0C blockNumber = 1` · content at **+0x20** · one block **`CLT1`**, size **0x420**, and **`0x20 + 0x420 = 0x440 = EOF exactly`** · `+0x08 u16 = 256` entry count, `+0x0A` pad · **`RGBA8[256]` at file `0x2C`–`0x42C`, ALL 256 alphas `0xFF`** · trailing `0x42C`–`0x440` = **20 bytes zero padding to the 32-byte boundary**. · **⚠ IT INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMS THE 32-BYTE-UNIT FINDING ON A SECOND FORMAT.** `34 × 32 = 1088 = len(color.bmc)`, exactly as `0x4E0B × 32 = 639328 = len(zel_00.bmg)`. **Different magic, different file type, same size convention — so it is a rule of the family, not a quirk of one file**, and the trailing zero padding on both is the same alignment. **I measured them separately and they agree.** · **THE PALETTE: 256 entries, 9 distinct colours** — `FFFFFF` white · `FF6400` orange · `00FF00` green · `7878FF` blue · `FFFF3C` yellow · `00FFFF` cyan · `FF00FF` magenta · `828282` grey · `FF8000` bright orange; **indices 9–255 are all white.** · **THIS IS WHAT `defaultColor` AND `mNowColor` INDEX INTO.** INF1 carries `defaultColor` as a **u8 at 0x0E** and `dMesg_tSequenceProcessor` carries `mNowColor` — **a byte-indexed palette is exactly the shape both need**, and the colour tag in a message selects a slot rather than carrying an RGB. **And note the donor's own out-of-range behaviour: 9–255 resolve to WHITE, so a bad index degrades to white rather than to garbage. That is donor behaviour to PRESERVE, not a guard for anyone to add** (DN-10: the native system already handles it). · **THE PORT CONSEQUENCE, concrete and cheap: the archive must be consumed WHOLE.** `bmgres.arc` is already served from the user's ISO at `res/Object/` and the §1024 `res/Msg/` collision removal does not touch that — **so this member is already on disc and needs NO new serve, only a consumer that reads it.** The cost of missing it is not a crash; it is every coloured word in the game silently rendering white, which is the class of bug that ships. · **SPEC: `docs/state/ww-message-donor-spec.md` §7.9** (and §7.6 for the re-derivation that found it). **Still UNKNOWN and flagged, not assumed: which tag code selects a colour slot** — that is the tag interpreter, and it lives in NONMATCHING `processor.cpp` / `d_msg.cpp`. | History/Bridge, MGCL colour table decoded 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16
- [x] FOUNDRY | **FINDINGS REPORT — WW MESSAGE DECODE, AND THE ONE THING I CANNOT DO WITHOUT AN INSTRUMENT FROM YOU: VERIFY A NONMATCHING RECONSTRUCTION AGAINST THE BINARY (History/Bridge, tale §1011).** · **WHAT YOUR TOOLS FOUND ONCE I ACTUALLY RAN THEM** (the user had to ask before I did — recorded in my `port_preflight` row): `decomp_status` -> **3 MATCHED / 7 NONMATCHING / 93 markers** on the message corpus; `port_preflight` leg 1 -> **`not MATCHED: verbatim port impossible; say so in the queue row (§801)`**, which answered a question I had wrongly escalated to the user as open; `ww_disc.rarc_list` -> **a second archive member no census contains**. · **THE FINDING THAT VALIDATES THE WHOLE `decomp_status` SPLIT, and I want it on your record because it is the first time NONMATCHING has been shown to be WRONG rather than merely unproven:** two donor structs describe the same INF1 header — `JUTMesgInfo` (**MATCHED**) puts the entry table at **0x10**, `mesg_info` (**NONMATCHING** `f_op_msg_mng.cpp`) puts it at **0x14**. **Measured on the user's disc: 0x10 gives 40/40 in-range ascending DAT1 offsets; 0x14 gives 8/40 and reads `mMsgNo<<16|mItemPrice` as an offset.** The reconstruction is the one that drifted. **A deliberately-wrong offset (0x12) was scored alongside both and came last (1/40), so the test could have returned INCONCLUSIVE and did not.** · **THE GENERAL FORM, which is yours to decide what to do with: A NONMATCHING TU'S STRUCT DEFINITIONS ARE NOT NEUTRAL — THEY CAN BE ACTIVELY WRONG, AND ITS SIBLINGS ARE THEREFORE SUSPECT AS A SET.** `mesg_header`, `mesg_info`, `mesg_data` are declared together at `f_op_msg_mng.cpp:28-42`; **one of the three is now proven wrong, so the other two carry no more weight than it did.** `decomp_status` today rates a TU; **nothing rates the STRUCTS inside it, and layout is what a fill consumes.** · **THE ASK, and it is the blocker on my remaining work: WHAT INSTRUMENT VERIFIES A RECONSTRUCTION AGAINST THE BINARY?** Everything lef