- [ ] HOUSING | **ROUTED WORK — WHAT THE MESSAGE DECODE HANDS YOU, AND THE SIX THINGS THAT WILL SILENTLY BREAK A FILL. Nothing here is buildable yet; this is so the implementation half is not designed against wrong facts (History/Bridge, tale §1011).** **SPEC: `docs/state/ww-message-donor-spec.md`.** · **YOUR SIX TRAPS, each measured, each failing SILENTLY rather than loudly:** **(1) INF1's entry table is at 0x10, NOT 0x14** — the NONMATCHING `mesg_info` says 0x14 and is wrong; wrong choice misaligns every message by four bytes and resolves strings to garbage, no error. **(2) The BMG header size field @0x08 is in 32-BYTE UNITS** (`0x4E0B × 32 = 639328`) — bounds-check it as bytes and you reject nearly every valid message. **Confirmed on a second format** (`color.bmc`, `34 × 32 = 1088`), so it is a family convention. **(3) FOUR addressing schemes, not one:** engine `(groupID<<16)|messageIndex` · WW game `(groupID<<8)|mResMsgNo` · tag `(group8<<16)|code16` · message **IDs** via MID1. **Do not feed a `getMesgNumber()` result to `setMessageCode()`.** **(4) Text is MULTIBYTE** — `TResourceContainer` carries an encoding AND an `mIsLeadByteFunc` predicate; an ASCII assumption corrupts every wide glyph. **(5) Nesting depth is HARD-CAPPED AT 4 and `push()` does NOT bounds-check** — `IsStorable()` is the caller's obligation; deeper jump/branch/select overruns the array. **(6) Tag dispatch is TWO-TIER** — subclass `do_tag` gets first refusal, base `do_tag_` takes what it declines; collapse it and WW's own tags are swallowed or dropped, and neither path errors. · **THE COLOUR TABLE IS A DELIVERABLE, NOT A DETAIL: `bmgres.arc` has TWO members — `zel_00.bmg` AND `color.bmc` (`MGCL`).** A byte-indexed `RGBA8[256]` palette, 9 distinct colours, indices 9-255 white. **INF1's `defaultColor` (u8 @0x0E) and `mNowColor` index into it.** **Already served from the user's ISO — no new serve needed, only a consumer.** Miss it and every coloured word renders white. **Preserve the donor's out-of-range behaviour (degrades to white); do not add a guard — DN-10, the native system already handles it.** · **THE GOOD NEWS, and it should shape your design: WW's MESSAGE PRESENTATION IS DATA-DRIVEN.** `JMSMesgEntry_c` (0x18, stride confirmed against the disc) carries `mTextboxType`, `mDrawType`, `mTextboxPosition`, `mItemImage`, `mTextAlignment`, `mInitialSound`, `mInitialCamera`, `mInitialAnimation`, plus `mNextMsgNo` — **chaining is DATA, not control flow.** A faithful reader gets WW-correct presentation out of the user's own bytes without reimplementing the logic that chose it. · **AND THE SHELL HAS SOMEWHERE TO STAND ON VANILLA — measured, both images:** `fopDwTg_ToDrawQ` · `fopDwTg_DrawQTo` · `fopDwTg_Init` · `fpcLf_DrawMethod` · `fpcBs_MakeOfType` all **SAFE, one entry each, on fork AND vanilla upstream**. **CAVEAT: `--symbol` SAFE is LEG 1 OF 3** — it does not prove a call site reaches it (inlining) or that the loader can bind it (MULTI_NAME needs `--find` on the user's image). **Do not approve a binding on that table alone.** · **WHAT YOU CANNOT START ON, and why:** all five BUILD-QUEUE rows stay `BLOCKED—PASS 2` and out of MIN-BATCH-5. `port_preflight` returns **`not MATCHED: verbatim port impossible (§801)`** for this corpus — 7 of 10 TUs NONMATCHING. **The tag interpreter, the `d_*` layer and the 7,637-line manager are reconstructions, so the BEHAVIOUR half of this system is not yet donor-established.** Design against the layout facts above; do not design against anything sourced from a NONMATCHING body until it is verified. · **`d_message_paper.cpp` is effectively UNAVAILABLE — 55 markers in 325 lines.** If paper messages are in scope, say so and it becomes its own problem. | History/Bridge, routing the message decode 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16
