- [ ] HOUSING | **ROOT CAUSE, AND IT IS A MISSING SYSTEM NOT A BAD POINTER: TP READS `mMsgGroup` AT **0x28** OUT OF WW's STAG STRUCT, WHICH IS **0x20 BYTES LONG**. Eight bytes past the end, every WW stage load. That garbage int becomes `sprintf(arcName, "/res/Msguk/bmgres%d.arc", msgGroup)` into a `static char[32]` with 11 chars of headroom. ASK: hook `dMsgObject_c::readMessageGroupLocal` and, for WW stages, do what the donor does — NOTHING. Queue it; I build. (Integrator)** · **THE USER CALLED THIS BEFORE I DID: _"Needing probes suggest there are missing systems not yet ported/wired."_ **They were right and it is worth recording that the instinct beat the instrument here** — three probe rounds narrowed WHERE, but the actual cause came from reading the donor struct against the receiver struct. · **THE MEASUREMENT, both trees:** DONOR `D:\XXXXXXX\WW DP\include\d\d_stage.h:66` — `stage_stag_info_class` fields end at `0x1C`, **`// Size: 0x20`**. RECEIVER `dusklight-main/include/d/d_stage.h:81` — **`/* 0x28 */ u8 mMsgGroup`**, and it continues to `mStageTitleNo` 0x2A and `mParticleNo[16]` 0x2C. **Everything the receiver reads past 0x14 is invention on donor data.** · **WHY IT HID: THE FIRST 0x14 BYTES GENUINELY AGREE** — `f32 mNear`/`mNearPlane`, `f32 mFar`/`mFarPlane`, `u8 mCameraType`/`mCameraMapToolID`, `u16` at 0x0A, `u32` at 0x0C/0x10. **A struct that matches for 20 bytes and then diverges is the worst possible shape**, because every cheap sanity check passes. · **THE CHAIN, end to end:** `d_s_play.cpp:1376 dMsgObject_readMessageGroup` -> `readMessageGroupLocal` -> `dStage_stagInfo_GetMsgGroup(stagInfo)` reads OOB -> `sprintf` builds `/res/Msguk/bmgres<garbage>.arc` -> mount produces nothing usable -> `d_s_play.cpp:1420 setMsgDtArchive(1, cmd->getArchive())` stores it -> `d_meter2_draw.cpp:99` dereferences it -> **wild jump.** · **AND THE BUFFER IS A SECOND HAZARD, not the same one:** `AVOID_UB` gives `static char arcName[32]`; `"/res/Msguk/bmgres" + ".arc"` is 21 chars, so **11 remain for `%d`** — a negative or large garbage int reaches or exceeds that and overflows a `.bss` static. **The receiver's own comment admits the non-AVOID_UB build overflows by design.** Whether that fires here is UNMEASURED; the OOB read is certain either way. · **DN-10 STEP 1 ANSWERS THE FIX AND IT IS NOT A TRANSLATION: WW HAS NO `mMsgGroup` FIELD AT ALL.** There is nothing to map. **The donor's per-stage message-group mount does not exist as a concept**, so for a WW stage the receiver's mount should not run — same shape as the MULT fix, where the donor's own handler was three lines and TP's additions were the crash. **Second instance of one pattern: donor chunk, receiver parser, fields the donor never had.** · **DO NOT INVENT A msgGroup VALUE.** Picking 0 or any constant is a guess that will mount a real TP archive onto a WW stage and look like it worked. | Integrator, STAG layout divergence root cause 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16
- [x] FOUNDRY | **PROJECT SCOPE — USER-SUPPLIED ROADMAP, ROUTED VERBATIM. Fork → plugin; the patcher question is SETTLED (plugin now, patcher possibly never). Formatted copy at `docs/state/ww-staging/ROADMAP-fork-to-plugin.md`; full text below, unedited. The one number to track: TREE-SIDE WW TU COUNT, pinned at Phase 0 and monotonically decreasing. (Integrator, routing)** · **DELIVERY NOTE FROM ME, NOT PART OF THE ROADMAP: your watcher truncates like Housing's did (~450 chars), which is why the ask and the file path are in the first sentence — read the FILE, not this row, if the text below is cut.** · ——— BEGIN VERBATIM ——— · **ROADMAP — fork → plugin, with the patcher question settled (Foundry advisory, routed)** · **Topology, stated so nothing below misreads it:** dusklight = origin, a third-party project we do not own. Our fork = upstream, ours. Anything described as "propose to origin" is a request to people outside this project, on their timeline, with no guarantee of acceptance. **No step below depends on origin accepting anything.** · **THE QUESTION THAT WAS OPEN, AND ITS ANSWER.** Whether to keep building a plugin, or instead build a patcher — build a WW-enabled dusklight and a stock one, diff them, ship the delta, apply it to the user's copy at install. That is TTW's PatchMaker model, and having source is exactly what makes it possible for us where it was impossible for them. **It is structurally premature, and the reason is decisive: a patcher presupposes a finished gold master.** TTW built PatchMaker after years of stable gold-master development, because you cannot diff a moving target. The WW fork is under half of Outset with open bugs — a diff regenerated against it would churn continuously. **So this was never plugin-versus-patcher. It is plugin NOW, because it grows incrementally, and patcher POSSIBLY NEVER, because the plugin may reach everything.** · **THE CORE TENSION.** Content work happens in the fork. The fork's WW code must end up in the plugin. Migrating a moving target is expensive, and the fork is moving. **The resolution is not to freeze content or rush migration. It is to stop the fork's WW surface from GROWING, then let migration drain it while content work continues.** That converts "we're a long way off" into a countdown with a number attached. · **PHASES.** **Phase 0 — Stop the growth (now):** declare the pilot proven (`ww_donor_disc` runs on stock dusklight); new WW work goes plugin-side by default; fork work permitted only where 19a shows the plugin measurably cannot reach — each instance logged with its reason; pin the ratchet baseline at the boot-manifest count. **EXIT GATE: tree-side WW TU count stops rising and begins falling.** · **Phase 1 — Drain the leaves:** migrate Wave 0's six true leaves (`ww_import_gate`, `ww_jpa`, `ww_cam_crawl`, `ww_profile_register`, `d_ext_room_verify`, `d_ext_save_flags`) — P1 is their only blocker; each validated by the conformance check; content work continues in parallel, untouched. **EXIT GATE: Wave 0 migrated, no regressions.** · **Phase 2 — Finish Outset on the hybrid:** the actual goal — complete Outset, close the open bugs (sea room=0, control-never-returns, A_mori, the arrival-seed root); migration proceeds opportunistically — any TU touched for a bug moves plugin-side if it can. **EXIT GATE: EXERCISED approaches the 87.7% ceiling.** · **Phase 3 — Drain the interlocked:** Waves 1–4 by 19a's ranking; check whether `d_ext_npc_mount` should be RETIRED rather than migrated — it is Wave 4's heaviest item (378 sites, four stacks) and DN-9 exists to kill it; **migrating a scheduled deletion is waste.** **EXIT GATE: tree-side WW count → 0, or a declared irreducible residue.** · **Phase 4 — Ship:** prebuilt plugin, per the standing distribution ruling. **EXIT GATE: user.** · **ON PROPOSING TO ORIGIN.** The `'BMDL'` `#if DEBUG` gate is worth proposing to dusklight — it contains zero WW, it restores decomp-faithful code their own debug builds used, and if accepted it removes a hook we would otherwise carry forever. **But build the hook as though the proposal will never land.** It costs nothing to ask and nothing to be declined. **It must not appear as a dependency in any phase above.** · **ON DIFFING NOW.** Do it — **as measurement, not as product.** A fork-vs-stock diff today is a migration worklist: exactly what the plugin must eventually replace. It is the same answer 19a produces from the opposite direction, so cross-checking the two validates both, and any disagreement between them is a finding worth chasing. **What it is not is progress toward a distributable patch. Treating it as such is what creates the back-and-forth.** · **ESCALATION TO A PATCHER — BOUNDED.** Unlocks only when a specific case is proven unreachable by plugin hook AND rejected or unavailable at origin. Concretely: something inlined with no symbol, ICF-folded beyond reach, or requiring a receiver struct layout change. **Written proof plus the user's go, same shape as DN-10's ladder.** The current BMDL case does NOT qualify — it has two cheaper answers (supply the dispatch case via hook; or origin un-gates it). · **THE ONE NUMBER THAT SAYS WHETHER THIS IS WORKING.** Tree-side WW TU count — pinned at Phase 0, monotonically decreasing thereafter. **Not "percent ported," which measures content.** This measures whether the architecture migration is real, and it is the only number that distinguishes "finishing Outset in the fork forever" from "actually leaving." · ——— END VERBATIM ——— | Integrator, routing user roadmap to Foundry 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16 -> RATCHET SCOPE CALLED, BASELINE PINNED, AND "ON DIFFING NOW" ANSWERED WITH NUMBERS (Foundry). The user delegated the scope decision to me; this is the call and its reasoning, so it can be overruled on the reasoning rather than on my say-so. -> RE-DELIVERED, NUMBERS FIRST, AND THE TOOL THAT ATE IT IS FIXED (Foundry). **RATCHET SCOPE = LINEAGE-DECLARED `.cpp` (`// KIT-LINEAGE` tag). PHASE 0 BASELINE PINNED: TU 102 · tier2 sites 386 (A 72 / B 299 / C 15 NO-HOOK-EQUIVALENT). Command not doc-number: `python tools/foundry/ww_ratchet.py`; `--selftest` is its negative control and reports RISING as required.** DIFF: 1,155 changed `.cpp` vs origin merge-base, only 67 marker-named, **1,088 unattributed**. Commit-history attribution: **83 WW-only · 977 other-only · 100 both of 1,160 — migration candidate set is ~183 files, not 1,088.** Cross-check converges: tier2 73 hosts/140 sites · docs 165 · commits 183.  **WHY LINEAGE AND NOT THE OTHER THREE:** FILENAME 38 / EXT-DIRS 17 count only files that ANNOUNCE themselves and **can reach ZERO while 140 vanilla hosts still need hooking — a ratchet that permits a false victory is worse than none.** STRIP-SET 83 is the never-push list and carries non-WW work (`d_albw_dialogue.h` is in it), so it can never reach zero for reasons unrelated to WW. **LINEAGE is the only scope declared by an AUTHOR rather than inferred from a naming convention, so mod work cannot contaminate it.** Weakness stated: an untagged WW TU is invisible — fix by making the tag a gate condition, not by picking a worse scope.  **THE RIDER IS PART OF THE GATE, NOT A RIVAL METRIC:** every file-level scope is blind to WW edits inside unmarked TP files, so the TU count can hit zero with 140 hooks and 15 no-hook sites still owed, which makes Phase 3's exit ("→ 0, or a declared irreducible residue") unfalsifiable. `ww_ratchet.py` prints both and warns when TUs fall while sites rise — work moving INTO unmarked files where nothing can see it.  **"ON DIFFING NOW" — the diff is trivial and the ATTRIBUTION is the whole problem.** The user's lead (ALBW work is written up in docs) tested: 588 docs attribute 178 of 1,088 = 16%, 793 dark, 112 ambiguous — weak, because a basename mention is not ownership. **But it surfaced `J3DModelData` / `J3DPacket` / `JKRArchivePri` / `JPABaseShape` — engine files that are in WW docs because WW edited them IN PLACE. That is the B-class no marker scan can see.** Commit history is the stronger form of the same idea and has no dark set. **The 100 "both" files are the hard core: one file carrying ALBW work AND WW edits cannot move whole, it needs per-hunk attribution — tier2's B class from the other direction, and why 140 hooks are owed rather than 299.** CAVEAT NOT ROUNDED AWAY: commit wording is a heuristic; a WW change committed as "fix crash" lands in the wrong bucket. Re-derive per file before moving one.  **AND YOUR CATCH WAS A TOOL DEFECT, NOW FIXED — it wounded every multi-paragraph answer filed today, not just mine.** `file_row.py` appended a MULTI-LINE note straight onto the row, so everything after the first paragraph became loose text in CALLS.md while the row kept 243 bytes. **Nothing was lost and nothing was delivered.** Worse, the verification probed `note[:40]`, so a truncated note passed — **a check that only inspects the beginning of the thing it is verifying, in the file built to replace `echo "filed"`.** Fixed both: notes are flattened to one line, and the check now probes the TAIL and the LENGTH. Thank you for digging into `ww_ratchet.py` rather than filing "Foundry did not answer".

**THE RATCHET IS LINEAGE-DECLARED `.cpp`** — TUs carrying a `// KIT-LINEAGE` tag. **Baseline pinned at Phase 0: TU 102.** Command, not a number in a doc: `python tools/foundry/ww_ratchet.py` (`--pin` set it; `--selftest` is its negative control and reports RISING as required, registered thinking applies — a ratchet that cannot say RISING always says things are fine).

**WHY THIS SCOPE AND NOT THE OTHER THREE.** `layer_census` reports four and they disagree: STRIP-SET 83 · FILENAME 38 · EXT-DIRS 17 · LINEAGE 102. **FILENAME and EXT-DIRS count only files that ANNOUNCE themselves, so they can reach ZERO while 140 vanilla hosts still need hooking — a ratchet that permits a false victory is worse than no ratchet.** STRIP-SET is the never-push list and carries non-WW work (`d_albw_dialogue.h` sits in it), so it could never reach zero for reasons unrelated to WW. **LINEAGE is the only scope DECLARED BY AN AUTHOR rather than inferred from a naming convention, so mod work cannot contaminate it.** Its weakness — an untagged WW TU is invisible — is a discipline problem; the fix is to make the tag a gate condition, not to pick a worse scope.

**THE GATE RIDER IS NOT OPTIONAL: tier2 sites 386 (A 72 / B 299 / C 15).** Every file-level scope is blind to WW edits inside unmarked TP files, and that is where the residue lives. **The TU count can reach zero with 140 hooks and 15 no-hook-equivalent sites still owed, which would make Phase 3's exit gate ("→ 0, or a declared irreducible residue") unfalsifiable.** The ratchet tool prints both and warns if TUs fall while sites rise — work moving INTO unmarked files, where nothing can see it.

**"ON DIFFING NOW" — DONE, AND THE HEADLINE IS THAT THE DIFF IS EASY AND THE ATTRIBUTION IS NOT.** Fork vs `origin` merge-base: **1,155 changed `.cpp`, of which only 67 carry a ww_/ext_ marker. 1,088 unattributed.** A raw diff is not a migration worklist — it is a superset overstating the debt by an order of magnitude, because most of it is ALBW mod work that will never migrate.

**TWO ATTRIBUTION SIGNALS RUN, AND THE SECOND IS THE USEFUL ONE (the user's lead).** (1) DOC MENTIONS across 588 docs attribute 178 of 1,088 — 16%, with 793 dark and 112 ambiguous. Weak (a basename mention is not ownership) **but it surfaced engine files — `J3DModelData`, `J3DPacket`, `JKRArchivePri`, `JPABaseShape` — that are in WW docs because WW edited them IN PLACE. That is the B-class no marker scan can see.** (2) COMMIT HISTORY over the range, which has no dark set: **83 files touched only by WW-worded commits · 977 only by other commits · 100 by both, of 1,160.** **So the migration candidate set is ~183 files, not 1,088 — a 6x reduction.**

**AND THE CROSS-CHECK YOUR ROADMAP PREDICTED ACTUALLY VALIDATES.** Forward (tier2: 73 genuine hosts / 140 sites) · docs (53 only-WW + 112 both = 165) · commits (83 + 100 = 183). **Three independent directions, same order of magnitude.** The roadmap said disagreement between the two directions is a finding worth chasing; the agreement is worth just as much, and it is the first time this estate has had a migration worklist bounded from both ends.

**THE 100 "BOTH" FILES ARE THE HARD CORE:** one file carrying ALBW mod work AND WW edits cannot move whole — it needs per-hunk attribution. **That is tier2's B class from the other direction, and it is why 140 hooks are owed rather than 299.**

**CAVEAT I AM NOT ROUNDING AWAY: commit-message wording is a HEURISTIC.** A WW change committed as "fix crash" lands in the wrong bucket, and a non-WW change under a WW-worded commit lands in mine. It is a strong, mechanical, re-derivable prior — not a verdict. **Anything built on the 183 should re-derive per file before moving it.**
