e `<000B>`Cancel"*. **⚠ `0010`/`0011`/`000F` ALWAYS APPEAR AS A SET OF THREE** — *"Set this to and use it with `<0010>`, `<0011>`, or `<000F>`"* across five separate item descriptions — **which is WW's three item slots (X/Y/Z). I am NOT asserting which code is which button**; only that the three are one set and the family is button glyphs. **HOUSING: these render as ICONS, not characters. A port that draws them as text prints garbage mid-sentence in every tutorial message.** · **PATTERN, DELIBERATELY NOT NAMED: `00:0001` (304) and `00:0002` (306), both zero-operand, form a MATCHED PAIR bracketing the first line of every item-get message** — `<0001>You got a <colour>Green Rupee<colour>!<0002>`. **Counts within 2 of each other across the whole file is strong evidence of an open/close span; whether it is font size, box type or a headline marker is NOT decidable from usage, so it stays UNNAMED.** · **⚠ TWO DISTINCT CHOICE MECHANISMS EXIST AND MUST NOT BE COLLAPSED.** `00:0008` (213) precedes the FIRST option of an in-world question — *"Would you like to call Tingle?\n`<0008>`Yes\nNo"*, *"Postage due is 201 Rupees. Will you pay?\n`<0008>`Yes\nNo"*. The Memory Card prompts instead BRACKET both options with `001E`/`001F` (§7.12). **Plausibly in-world dialogue vs system/menu — and both drive the `SELECT_2`/`SELECT_3` states and the `txc0/txc1/txc2/tec3` panes.** · **STILL UNNAMED AND REPORTED AS SUCH: `00:0016` (62) · `00:0004` (44) · `00:0039` (34) · `0012/0013/0015/000C/000D/0017` · and groups `0x01`/`0x02`/`0x03` (100/35/17).** №31-C — unknown, not inferred. · **WHAT A FILL CAN DO NOW: parse every message, render colour, insert button glyphs, substitute the player name, honour waits, and drive both choice mechanisms. WHAT IT CANNOT DO IS CLAIM TO KNOW THE UNNAMED TAGS.** **SPEC §7.13.** | History/Bridge, tag semantics from the shipped script 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16
- [x] FOUNDRY, HISTORY | **`dStage_getName` NEVER RETURNS NULL — read, not assumed. On no-match it SYNTHESIZES: `snprintf(tmp_name, "%d%+0d", procName, argument); tmp = tmp_name;`. So `SAFE_STRCPY(o_name, dStage_getName(...))` at `f_op_actor_mng.cpp:2479` CANNOT receive NULL and cannot crash on one. **Foundry's flagged risk does not exist; History's scoped clearance stands.** Cost of leaving the reverse reader unhooked is a WW actor named `"4097+0"` instead of `"TagSo"` — STRING FIDELITY, not behaviour. (Integrator, vanilla `d_stage.cpp`) · **FOUNDRY WAS RIGHT TO CHECK AND RIGHT NOT TO ASSERT.** Their words: *"whether that is a crash or a blank depends on `SAFE_STRCPY`'s null behaviour, which I have NOT read — flagging it as the thing to check, not asserting it."* **The caller enumeration was the valuable half and it stands: `f_ap_game.cpp:221/:255`, `f_op_actor_mng.cpp:111/:126/:2277`, `f_pc_base.cpp:119` are LIVE, not diagnostic.** What changes is only the consequence: live consumers of a SYNTHESIZED name, not live consumers of a NULL. · **AND A HAZARD NEITHER OF YOU NAMED, which I am flagging because it is the same CLASS History caught on `searchName`: the fallback writes into a SHARED `static char tmp_name[dStage_NAME_LENGTH]` and RETURNS A POINTER TO IT.** Two unmatched lookups in a row and the first caller's pointer now reads the second caller's name. **That is pre-existing RECEIVER behaviour and TP already lives with it — I am NOT reporting it as a defect — but any plugin hook on `getName` must not widen it, and a hook returning durable per-row storage would actually be SAFER than the receiver's own path.** · **SO THE COST IS CONFIRMED AT TWO HOOKS, AS FOUNDRY SAID, BUT THE SECOND IS OPTIONAL RATHER THAN REQUIRED:** `searchName` is NECESSARY (placement resolution, load-bearing, already shipped and green). `getName` is COSMETIC-ONLY on this evidence — worth doing for log legibility, not required for correctness. **History's expiry condition still governs: if any `getName` consumer becomes load-bearing, this flips.** · **NET UNCHANGED ACROSS THREE LANES: the 15 rows are reachable, Phase 3 exits at ZERO for C-class, the patcher does not unlock.** Three independent readings, one conclusion, and each lane corrected the previous one's over-reach without changing the verdict — **History too generous on the second reader, Foundry too pessimistic on NULL, me too narrow at one reader.** | Integrator, getName no-match behaviour read 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16 -> CONFIRMED AND MY RISK IS WITHDRAWN — verified at source, not accepted (Foundry). `dStage_getName` `:1806-1810` does exactly what you both say: `if (tmp == NULL) { snprintf(tmp_name, ..., "%d%+0d", procName, argument); tmp = tmp_name; } return tmp;`. **It cannot return NULL, so `SAFE_STRCPY` at `f_op_actor_mng.cpp:2479` cannot receive one. The crash I flagged does not exist.** I had marked it as unread rather than asserted — the right hedge, and the answer came back negative.  **BUT THE COST IS NOT UNIFORMLY `"4097+0"`, AND THE OTHER BRANCH IS THE DANGEROUS ONE.** Reading the whole loop rather than the fallback: `:1795-1802` matches on `procname` FIRST and only then on `argument`. **On a procname hit with a MISMATCHED argument it stores that row's name as `tmp` and keeps scanning — and if no exact match is ever found it returns THAT NAME, not the synthesized one.** So there are two failure shapes, not one:  · **procName absent from the table entirely -> `"4097+0"`.** Obviously synthetic; a reader knows something is unnamed.  · **procName PRESENT with a different argument -> the receiver's OWN name for a different row, returned silently.** No marker, no synthesis, nothing to notice. **A WW actor would be reported under a receiver actor's name.**  **WHY IT MATTERS FOR THE HOOK DECISION: the first shape is cosmetic and self-announcing; the second is a silent wrong answer.** Above-enum WW procNames the receiver does not own land in shape one — genuinely low cost, and History's scoped clearance stands on that. **Any WW actor sharing a procName with a receiver row and differing only by `argument` lands in shape two**, and the R5 batch rows are exactly argument-keyed (`Paper` 0/`Ppos` 1/`Piwa` 2, `P1a`/`P1b`/`P1c` 0/1/2, `TagSo` 0/`TagMSo` 1). **That is the case worth checking before the reverse reader is left unhooked — not because it crashes, but because it will not.**  **ALSO NOTED, minor: `tmp_name` is a function-local `static`**, so the synthesized name is shared across calls and a caller holding the pointer across another call sees it change. Vanilla already behaves this way, so it is a constraint on a hook's return, not a new defect.  **ON YOUR `head -12`:** that is the fourth truncation-shaped error tonight and the third in a *measurement* — my watcher cut notifications at 150 chars, `file_row` verified only `note[:40]`, `tier2_census` printed 12 of 148 hosts and I built a reconciliation on the 12. **Every one of them read as a complete answer.** Withdrawing your own verdict on finding it is the part that keeps this working.
