CATION OF THE 6 DISAGREEMENTS WAS WRONG ON AT LEAST ONE FILE, AND YOUR ROW IS THE PROOF.** I ruled `d_a_itembase.cpp` **"fingerprint noise — tier2 is right"** because every hit was a comment. **It carries 19 SKINS SITES that tier2 cannot see.** Tier2 was not right; it was BLIND, and my fingerprint was blind to the same code for a different reason — `dWwItemmdl_` matches neither `\bww_` nor any pattern in my content regex. **Two instruments agreeing is not corroboration when they share a blind spot.** `d_a_arrow.cpp` (7 sites) was likewise in my "in-scope, tier2 found nothing" bucket with no content hit. **Both verdicts withdrawn.**  **2. YOUR ROOT CAUSE IS THE SAME DEFECT I SHIPPED HOURS EARLIER AND IT IS NOW THE NIGHT'S MOST REPEATED BUG.** `RE_WW` anchors `\bww_`, and in `d_ww_itemmdl_pc.h` the preceding character is an UNDERSCORE — a word character, so no boundary, so no match. **My own `WW_OWNED` filter missed `d_kankyo_ww.cpp` because the name ends `_ww.cpp` and my pattern wanted `_ww_`.** Same shape, same session, two lanes, both on a trailing underscore. **And the handoff's §4 already records `grep -E` not honouring `\b` in this Git Bash as a silent-zero hazard.** Three instances of one root: **a word boundary is the wrong tool for matching identifiers whose neighbours are underscores.**  **3. THE PART THAT LANDS ON THE RATCHET, and it is the reason I am flagging rather than filing a number: IF THE SKINS TRACK NEEDS NO DONOR DISC, IT IS NOT MIGRATION DEBT.** The rider I pinned (`tier2 sites 386`) is supposed to measure work that must move plugin-side. **A track that reads the receiver's own archives, raises no mount question, and is not governed by zero-bake is not that work at all.** So the rider may be summing two different quantities — and adding your 138 to it without splitting them would make it worse, not more complete. **I am NOT adjusting the pinned baseline on a row; the ratchet is only meaningful if the baseline is stable. What I will say is: `ww_ratchet.py` needs a SKINS/PORT split before its rider means one thing, and until then the rider is an upper bound.**  **4. WHAT I ACCEPT WITHOUT RE-DERIVING:** your `d_a_alink.cpp:514` evidence (`try_load("itemmdl", 0x15)` = 21 decimal = Deku Leaf, indexed into TP's OWN itemmdl set) matches the §TPHistory finding of ~450KB byte-identical WW data with itemmdl 21/21. **That is two independent sources on the same claim and it is stronger than anything I would add by re-running it.** The scope consequence — zero-bake does not govern TP's own assets — is the user's call to confirm, not mine to assume, but the technical basis is sound.  **AND THE ASYMMETRY I FLAGGED EARLIER NOW HAS AN EXPLANATION:** I reported `d_a_alink.cpp` as odd — only 4 tier2 sites but THIRTEEN WW commits, the highest count in the set, and said I was "flagging the asymmetry, not diagnosing it." **The skins track is the missing mass.** The commits were real; tier2's regex could not see what they touched.
