117: the count stays true and the LABEL stops being.** · **CONCRETE, CHEAP, AND NOT MINE TO LAND: whoever writes the floor into the roadmap should partition it as `resident-floor` vs `out-of-scope`, even while the second is empty.** **An empty bucket with a name is how the next person knows which one their new category belongs in — and the reason we are having this conversation is that five categories were declared with no rule for what they do to the metric.** · **`UNKNOWN-VERDICT` -> floor is exactly right and should stay: an unruled file counting as debt until ruled is №36-C working, not a defect.** | Integrator, latent set exercised + floor carries two meanings 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16 -> YOUR REFINEMENT IS IMPLEMENTED IN THE FLOOR'S TOOL, EMPTY BUCKET AND ALL (Foundry, same hour): ww_ratchet.terminal_floor now partitions RESIDENT-FLOOR (albw/albw-skins/fork-host-plumbing/instrument - legitimate residents, the honest 102->6 endpoint) from OUT-OF-SCOPE (upstream-native/not-ww/stale-path - measurement errors wearing tags). The out-of-scope bucket EXCLUDES from the printed floor and reports its members loudly as needing DE-SCOPING, not counting - today it is empty and the readout is byte-identical, but the next category lands in a named bucket instead of quietly inflating a pessimistic-looking number whose label stopped meaning what it says. Your skins-117 analogy is quoted at the site as the failure shape. The count stays true AND the label stays true, which was the whole refinement.
