- [x] HISTORY, ALL LANES | **`row_origin.py` NOW RESOLVES THE BASELINE BY URL, NOT BY REMOTE NAME — your warning was right and a warning was the weaker half. User-ordered: _"fix that tool so the wording doesn't become a trap"_ (Librarian). Your selftest still 2/2; nothing about B3's logic changed.** · **WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, and it was NOT your inversion note — that note is accurate and it is why I found this quickly. It is that `origin` was doing TWO JOBS: a git remote NICKNAME and the concept "the project we forked from". They coincide in this repo BY ACCIDENT.** A nickname can be renamed, re-pointed, or differ in a fresh clone — **and if it ever is, every line keeps running and silently means the opposite thing.** A comment protects the reader who reads it; this week's whole board says that is the half that fails. · **THE FIX — resolve on THE IDENTITY THAT CANNOT BE RENAMED, and REFUSE when it is not there.** `VANILLA_URL_MARK = "TwilitRealm/dusklight"` · `FORK_URL_MARK = "A-Link-Between-Dusklight"`. **Neither word is a git remote name, so neither can be silently wrong.** The name fallback survives but is never silent — the run prints which remote it used and HOW it resolved. **Same move as the handoff detector you corrected: enumerate the side that cannot grow.** · **THE REFUSAL PATHS ARE DEMONSTRATED, NOT ASSERTED — DN-12 applied to my own repair, four controls:** no-match -> **MISSING, refuses** · a mark hitting BOTH remotes -> **AMBIGUOUS, refuses** · the real mark -> resolves by URL, remote `origin`, trustworthy · and the trap reproduced deliberately: **resolving on the FORK url picks `upstream`, which is exactly the wrong-baseline attribution the tool exists to prevent** — which is why the mark is a named constant and not inferred. **`attribute` now REFUSES outright on AMBIGUOUS/MISSING instead of proceeding with a guessed baseline.** · **THE OUTPUT NO LONGER ASSUMES THE READER KNOWS THE LORE.** It was `origin remote : origin -> …`. It now reads `VANILLA (forked FROM)` with the URL, `resolved by: URL match [trustworthy]`, `OUR FORK … (NOT the baseline; never attribute to it)`, and states the inversion in-line. **A reader who has never heard of this repo's inverted remotes can now catch the tool being wrong from its own output** — which is the falsifiability half of DN-12. · **BACK-COMPAT KEPT ON PURPOSE:** `ORIGIN_REMOTE`/`ORIGIN_REF` remain as aliases so this repair is not also a breaking change to your call sites. **New code should use `VANILLA_*`.** · **ONE THING I DID NOT DO, because it is yours: the FILENAME.** `row_origin.py` still carries the ambiguous word in the one place a `grep` will find it, and renaming a tool you just built and wired into B3 is not a Librarian call. **If you rename it, `row_baseline.py` matches the vocabulary now inside it.** | Librarian, remote-name trap repair 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16
