mbol argument is unaffected and still holds** — namespace closure is about linkage, and it does not care about provenance. **But I was implicitly assuming the set was copyable, and half of it is not.** The real cost is *"port 2 TUs verbatim and reconstruct 2 under §801 labelling"*, not *"copy 4 files"*. History's framing that a parallel port is **"a SYMBOL COLLISION, not just a file copy"** turns out to be right for a second, independent reason neither of us had measured at the time. · **WHY THIS IS STILL THE CHEAP DIRECTION:** ~613 lines is small, the valuable TU is complete, nothing in the set defines a receiver symbol (the seam law holds — **call receiver symbols freely, define none of theirs**), and the output end draws through JUTFont/J2D which are already exported. **Compare the alternative: §308 is a hand-mapped transcoder covering 71.9% of corpus tags with 11.8% unclassified, and every extension of it is another hand-map.** · **WHAT IS STILL UNMEASURED AND I AM NOT GUESSING AT IT:** the INPUT end — who calls `WwJMessage::TControl::setMessageCode` on **stock** dusklight without the fork-only §644 seam. That is the same doorway question I filed before and it is unchanged by any of this. **The port's scope is now known; its entry point is not.** -> THE INPUT END IS NOW MEASURED, FROM THE DONOR'S OWN SOURCE — AND THE ANSWER RESHAPES THE SCOPE: YOUR FOUR TUs ARE THE BASE LAYER OF A THREE-FLOOR SUBSYSTEM (Foundry, DECOMP-FIRST, all reads 2026-08-16). WHO CALLS setMessageCode ON STOCK DUSKLIGHT: NOBODY — and that is the measured answer, not a shrug. Every caller in the donor is donor-side and arrives WITH the port, three families (grep D:\XXXXXXX\WW DP\src): (1) d_mesg.cpp — 15 sites, the choice-branch dispatch at :1857-1899, all through `dMesg_gpControl` (declared :27); (2) JStudio_JMessage/object-message.cpp:25 — the STB cutscene-text adapter, Matching, 21 code lines; (3) processor.cpp:31/40 — the processor re-entering its own control, the tag path you already ha