ved `0x01000000`)**, palette base **0x2C**, 1024 bytes, 20 trailing. **I read that field, log it, and do NOT interpret it or gate on it** — naming it unidentified beats inventing a meaning. · **BUILT, in my own TU:** `wwMessageColor_open/openFromArc/get/distinctCount/isOpen/close` in `ww_message.cpp`. **All 256 entries are read.** In the shipped file indices 9-255 are white and only 0-8 are distinct (verified: `entries 9..255 all white == True`, `distinct in 0..8 == 9`) — **but that is an observation about THIS FILE, not a rule about the format, and hardcoding "9 then white" would repeat the very mistake one level down.** The RARC walk was **extracted, not duplicated**: `wwMessage_openFromArc` and the new colour opener now share one `rarcFindMember`, because this file's own header says re-deriving that arithmetic would be a third implementation of something already right twice. Extraction only — all six refusal paths, bounds and log events are the originals. · **SELF-PROVING AT BOOT, not asserted:** `[WwMsg] selfproof_color` prints `distinct`, `idx0/1/3/8` and the hardcoded `idx1` beside them, so the disagreement is visible in the log rather than living in this row. All three TUs `cl /Zs /std:c++20` EXIT=0. Queued, not built (§839). · **WHAT I HAVE NOT DONE, and it is the half that changes pixels:** `kWwColor` is still what §308 reads. **Swapping the consumption site is a RECEIVER edit on a WW-shared path and I have queued it rather than made it** — and it should land with the WW-scope guard, not as a global change. Until then this is a proven-correct table that nothing draws from yet. -> **BUILT AND STAGED — AND THE LOCATION OF THIS VIOLATION IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING: THE INVENTED PALETTE IS FORK CODE, AND THE DONOR-FAITHFUL FIX LANDED PLUGIN-SIDE (Integrator).** · **TRACED IT MYSELF: the hardcoded table is `kWwColor[9]` at **`src/d/d_ext_dmesg.cpp:313`** — FORK. Your donor-read replacement is in **`mods-src/ww_donor_disc/ww_message.cpp`** — PLUGIN.** **So on an