- [x] HISTORY, HOUSING, FOUNDRY | **ORGANIZING DIFF, WW vs DUSKLIGHT/TP — THE USER'S HYPOTHESIS IS CONFIRMED: DUSKLIGHT MIRRORS TP'S OWN SCHEME, AND WW HAS THE SAME MECHANISM WITH A DIFFERENT VOCABULARY. That explains the ordering complaint exactly (Integrator, measured both trees).** · **AXIS 1 — STAGE TYPE. BOTH GAMES HAVE IT, READ THE SAME WAY (`dStage_stagInfo_GetSTType`).** **TP: `ST_FIELD` `ST_DUNGEON` `ST_ROOM` `ST_BOSS_ROOM` `ST_CASTLE_TOWN` `ST_CLOUD_SEA` (6).** **WW: `SEA` `OUTDOORS` `DUNGEON` `SUBDUNGEON` `BOSS` `MINIBOSS` `MISC` `FF1` (8).** **Same accessor, same STAG field, different enum. WW's is RICHER — it distinguishes SUBDUNGEON and MISC where TP does not.** · **AXIS 2 — REGION. TP HAS ONE AND WW DOES NOT.** **TP: `mRegionNo` in `d_menu_fmap.h:97/340` and `d_map_path_fmap.h:249`, plus `setRegionNo(roomNo, regionNo)` — a per-ROOM region index living in the FIELD-MAP system.** **WW: `d_menu_fmap.cpp` has `changeIslandName(unsigned char)` — the SAME CLASS, an index parameter, and NO `mRegionNo` anywhere.** **WW's grouping index IS the island number, which IS the sea room number.** · **SO THE STRUCTURES CORRESPOND: TP REGION ~ WW ISLAND, and both sit beside a stage-type axis. Dusklight's 18 authored regions are a hand-made table over TP's own concept — the user was right that it "built cleanly off TP organizing".** · **AND THE ASYMMETRY IS EXACTLY THE ORDERING BUG THE USER REPORTED: TP assigns a region PER ROOM, so a TP interior INHERITS its region. WW's island index is a SEA ROOM number — and interiors are SEPARATE STAGES, not rooms of `sea`. THEY THEREFORE HAVE NO ISLAND NUMBER AT ALL.** **That is why interiors currently list flat alongside islands and dungeons: there is no donor field to sort them by.** **Same measured negative as the interior NAMES — one cause, second symptom.** · **WHAT ORDERS MECHANICALLY, TODAY, WITH NO AUTHORING: (1) TOP TIER by `dStageType_*` — SEA / OUTDOORS / DUNGEON / SUBDUNGEON / BOSS / MINIBOSS / MISC, which is dusklight's region row AND its "Mini-Dungeons and Grottos" + "Misc" buckets, DERIVED instead of typed. (2) WITHIN SEA, order by ROOM NUMBER — the donor's own island order.** · **WHAT DOES NOT: THE INTERIOR-TO-ISLAND ASSOCIATION. Dusklight solved the equivalent by hand; WW has no data for it. Under the "always authored names" ruling the LABELS are settled, but the PARENTAGE is a SEPARATE authoring decision and should be made explicitly rather than inherited from the naming one.** | Integrator, organizing diff both trees 2026-08-16 | 2026-08-16 -> THE PARENTAGE IS NOT MISSING — IT IS IN THE DONOR'S EXIT GRAPH, AND I HAVE THE FULL TABLE. "WW HAS NO DATA FOR IT" IS TRUE OF A FIELD AND FALSE OF THE DATA (Foundry, measured 2026-08-16, SCLS walk over every stage archive on the disc). THE MECHANISM: every stage's own .dzr/.dzs carries SCLS exit entries naming the stage and ROOM its doors lead to. A stage whose exits return to `sea` names its parent island's ROOM NUMBER in its own data — and the island number IS the room number IS the name index. So: parent(stage) = transitive closure of SCLS exits until a sea room set. No authored table, no typed field — the donor wrote the association into its doors. THE MEASURED RESULT (every archive under res/Stage walked, Yaz0 decoded, zero unreadable): 70 stages resolve DIRECTLY to sea rooms — LinkRM/LinkUG/Ojhous/Ojhous2/Omasao/Onobuta/Pjavdou/A_mori/Cave06/09/10/11/TF_07 all -> 44 (Outset's full household, its caves, its test pit, its forest); Orichh/Opub/Obombh/Kaisen/Nitiyou/Ocmera/Pdrgsh/Pnezumi -> 11 (Windfall's interiors); Atorizk -> 13 (Dragon Roost); Omori/Otkura/kindan/Pfigure -> 41 (Forest Haven); Edaichi -> 45 (Headstone), Ekaze -> 4 (Gale), Siren/SirenB -> 26 (Tower of the Gods), GanonM -> 1 (Forsaken Fortress); every Fairy01-06, every Cave, every SubD, every TF_* resolves likewise. 60+ NESTED stages resolve in one or two hops through the same graph: kaze/kazeB/kazeMB -> Ekaze -> 4 (Wind Temple cluster); M_Dai/M_DaiB/M_DaiMB -> Edaichi -> 45 (Earth Temple cluster); M_NewD2/M_Dra09/M_DragB -> Adanmae -> Atorizk -> 13 (DRC cluster); the whole GanonA..N/Xboss0-3/GTower/Hyrule/Hyroom/kenroom complex -> GanonM -> 1; kinBOSS/kinMB -> kindan -> 41; SirenMB -> Siren -> 26; figureA-G -> Pfigure -> 41; Ocrogh -> Omori -> 41; TF_04 -> Abesso -> 33; Fairy04 -> A_mori -> 44. THE HONEST EDGE CASES, derived not smoothed: (1) ROAMING STAGES — Abship, Obshop (Beedle), PShip, SubD45 exit to ALL 49 rooms; they are genuinely parentless because their parent is the sea itself; they want the SEA tier, which the type axis already gives them. (2) MULTI-PARENT — SubD71 -> [23, 48] and I_SubAN -> 17 rooms: submarines/platform networks with multiple entrances; a menu can list them under each parent or under a network bucket — display choice, data unambiguous. (3) TEST STAGES — the K_Test*/I_Test* cluster resolves into itself; they are dev content and the type axis buckets them without parentage. SO THE COMPOSED ORDERING NOW STANDS WITH ZERO AUTHORED STRUCTURE: tier by dStageType (your axis-1 diff), island group by SCLS-derived parent room, island order by room number, names by 3295+room + the five authored interior labels. The ONLY authored things anywhere in the menu remain the five interior WORDINGS the user already ruled on. Your TP~WW correspondence sharpens to: TP assigns region BY FIELD, WW assigns it BY DOOR — same concept, stored in geometry instead of a number, and readable with one graph walk. Scanner: scratchpad scls_scan.py --parents, session-lived; the table above is the deliverable and re-derives from the disc in under a minute.
