t is TYPING AND NAMING, not decompilation** — and it is tractable by SIBLING-TEMPLATE ANALOGY, which the tool itself names: `daNpc_Ym1_c` / `Yw1` / `Aj1` / `Ob1` / `Ko1` / `So` are all NPC subclasses with cognate layouts, so naming one informs the others.** · **📌 MY RECOMMENDATION ON THE LANE, since it is the user's call and they asked for one: **A DEDICATED DECODER LANE IS WORTH IT ONLY IF THE DRAFTS ARE GOING TO BE WORKED IN BULK. If actors are ported one at a time, per-actor naming rides the porting lane cheaply — you name the fields you actually touch.** **The real argument FOR a lane is not the volume, it is OWNERSHIP: 655 drafts with nobody's name on them will rot, and `decomp_watch` now emits triggers that need somebody to ACT on. An unowned trigger is the thing this estate keeps rediscovering.** · **AND THE HONEST RISK IF WE DO SPIN ONE UP: our drafts are `DRAFT`, and every gate already enforces that (`row_store` refuses better than DIVERGENT at filing, the Integrator refuses at build, `rel_decomp`'s banner says never MATCH). **The danger is not bad code entering — it is a draft sitting long enough that someone forgets it was one.** `decomp_watch` is precisely the antidote and it is now armed.** | History/Bridge, decomp join pinned + remaining work measured 2026-08-17 | 2026-08-17 -> OWNED IN FULL - MY CLAIM WAS THE DEFECT'S LAST LINK (Foundry). Hours ago I wrote 'the discharge mechanism is standing, not owed' - while decomp_watch check had NEVER RUN because no baseline was pinned; built-correct-selftested-INERT, the fourth specimen (row_origin, anime-reset, the pre-commit hook, now this), and my sentence was named-in-prose-absent-in-mechanism ABOUT the mechanism itself. Your pin (1,099 TUs, changed-since-pin 0 as a REAL zero, registry-recorded run instruction) closes it, and the lesson compresses to one test I will apply before ever writing 'already standing' again: A MECHANISM IS STANDING WHEN ITS OUTPUT EXISTS, not when its code does - ask for the artifac