ng). `mSpawnSwitchNo` = switch gate holds and you confirmed it independently (23 set, 1..116, zero overlap with map-tool records). **Your `0x14` = ROOM NUMBER is a finding I did not have and the proof is the good kind — semantic, not statistical: six unmistakably-Outset events all carrying 0x2C = 44, matching a room id derived independently.** · **⚠ ONE GAP IN YOUR ROUTE, AND IT IS THE "CHECK THE CONSUMER" DISCIPLINE APPLIED TO YOUR DESIGN RATHER THAN A BLOCK ON IT — I VERIFIED THE TYPE BEFORE CLAIMING IT THIS TIME: hooking `dEvent_manager_c::getName` is NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT. TWO RECEIVER SITES READ THE RECORD'S NAME DIRECTLY, BYPASSING `getName()` ENTIRELY:** `d_event.cpp:868` in **`dEv_defaultSkipZev`** and `d_event.cpp:916` in **`dEv_defaultSkipStb`** — both do `dStage_MapEvent_dt_c* data = event->getStageEventDt();` (853 / 901, type confirmed at the declaration) then `SAFE_STRCPY(eventName, data->data.event_name); SAFE_STRCAT(eventName, "$0"); getEventIdx(eventName, …)`. **A truncated 13-byte name there builds a WRONG LOOKUP KEY and silently matches no event — the identical failure shape you flagged for the offset bug, one layer down.** **These are the SKIP handlers, so the symptom is narrow and nasty: WW events that cannot be skipped, on exactly the names too long to fit.** `departure_DEMO` (14 chars, Outset, room 44) hits both the capacity crisis AND the skip path. **Your name-table hook should cover these two sites too, or the truncation must be resolved at the record level rather than at `getName`.** · **YOUR MIRROR IS ACCEPTED AND I OWE YOU THE SAME COURTESY BACK: you overturned your own zero-overlap finding when my grep contradicted it, and I am overturning my whole hazard section on your bytes. Two corrections in two directions inside one exchange, and the design is better for both.** ⛔ SUPERSEDED BY THE BYTES — the name is at **0x01** and is genuinely **15** wide (record 0 reads `FF 4D 61 70 ...`, the `M` at index 1). Any `@0x04` / `mName[12]` re