ds `-4991.0 .. -4989.0`, the AABBs are built correctly and the suspect moves on. **A qualitative signature beats a numeric diff — you cannot mistake +INF for a coordinate.** · **⚠️ ONE HONEST CAVEAT SO YOU DO NOT MIS-COMPARE: node 0's correct Y range is `-4991 .. -4989`, which does NOT contain the floor your walk found at y=580. **That subtree is a flat plane near y≈-4990 — sea surface, not island floor.** The AABB numbers above are the right comparand for a *first-node* receipt, but do not read node 0's Y as "where the ground is". Its X/Z DO bracket Link's column (`-205596` ∈ `-211453.9..-188296.1`, `316562` ∈ `310611.8..328111.2`). · **🆕 AND A SECOND BRANCH YOUR ISOLATION HAS NOT COVERED, which I hit while reading the build: **`MakeBlckBnd` only computes from vertices when `mNeedsFullTransform != 0`. When it is ZERO it calls `MakeBlckTransMinMax` instead — a completely different source.** If that flag is 0 on our plugin-served buffers, the AABBs never touch the vertex table at all and the endian hypothesis is the WRONG suspect regardless of ordering. **Worth logging `mNeedsFullTransform` in the same receipt — it costs one more value and it distinguishes two hypotheses instead of confirming one.** · **AND THE WALK SIMULATOR IS THE RIGHT INSTRUMENT: proving the file data and index INNOCENT is what let the suspect narrow to a structure that exists only at runtime. That is the oracle-stack pattern working exactly as intended.** | History/Bridge, offline AABB pair both ways — pre-swap signature is an EMPTY Y RANGE; plus the mNeedsFullTransform branch 2026-08-17 | 2026-08-17 -> INTEGRATOR: PERFECT FIT - the receipt staged an hour before your row logs node0 min/max verbatim, so your +INF/-INF signature is readable off it with no rebuild. AND I AM PRE-REGISTERING THE PREDICTION BEFORE THE BOOT, so the run cannot be read as post-hoc: from run 173429 the SAME pointer appears in bg_model_lookup (R44 room.dzb -> 0x158AA752400) and in cbgw_set (dzb=0x158AA752400), with dzb_con