- [x] HOUSING/ENGINE, HISTORY/BRIDGE, FOUNDRY, DECODER, ALL LANES | **📘 THE USER PROPOSED A RECEIVER-SIDE **CATCH-ALL** — ONE GENERIC HANDLER FOR ALL WW ACTORS INSTEAD OF N PORTED ACTORS — AND IT IS A BETTER IDEA THAN A LABOUR SAVING: **IT DELETES THE AUTHORED-CONSTANT BUG CLASS ENTIRELY.** Design filed: `docs/state/ww-staging/SPEC-ww-generic-prop.md` (Integrator).** · **🎯 THE ARGUMENT THAT MATTERS, and it is not the line count: a generic handler must not be TOLD which resource index is the model and which is the collision — **it WALKS the arc and finds them by type** (data begins `J3D2` → model; name ends `.dzb` → collision). **That single decision deletes R4 for every actor at once.** Per-actor ports re-author those constants for every actor and get a fresh chance to be wrong each time; **tonight that chance was taken three times out of three** (dzb index 5-vs-7, the vtable-head invariant, `TEV_TYPE_BG0` 0-vs-1). **The catch-all authors them ZERO times.** · **✅ WHY IT IS FEASIBLE — the two ports already done prove the body is shared: a static prop's create/draw is the SAME FIVE STEPS every time (resLoad → model fetch+parse+create → dzb → `dBgW_NewSet(MOVE_BG)` → transS/ZXYrotM/scaleM → tev/setListBG/modelUpdateDL/setList). **`daLwood_c::_draw` IS step 5 verbatim; `akabe` is steps 1-4 with no model; neither actor's `_execute` does anything but increment a timer.** That shared body is the catch-all. · **⚠️ WHAT IT CANNOT DO, and the rule that makes that safe: it cannot supply BEHAVIOUR — state machines, animation, item drops, door logic, cutscene binding. A pot becomes a pot you cannot break. **THE RULE IS FAIL INERT, NEVER ABSENT: the handler must never return `cPhs_ERROR_e`.** An actor that cannot do its job still stands, holds its collision, and occupies its slot. **HOUSING — the fork already wrote this rule down at `d_a_ext_vegetation.cpp:995` for a different reason (the spawner FIFO identity shift); this generalises it.** And it makes real ports an UPGRADE PATH